DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
World-systems theory (Wallerstein) and dependency theory (Frank, Amin, and
their Latin American and African interlocutors) extend historical
materialism's core categories — mode of production, class, exploitation —
from the nation-state or single social formation to the capitalist
world-economy as a whole. Wallerstein's "modern world-system" treats the
world-economy inaugurated in the "long sixteenth century" as the true unit
of analysis, structured into core, periphery, and semi-periphery zones
linked by an international division of labor. Frank's "development of
underdevelopment" thesis holds that peripheral "satellite" regions are not
pre-capitalist survivals awaiting modernization but products of the same
world-capitalist process that produces metropolitan development — the two
are dialectically bound. Amin generalizes this into a theory of "unequal
exchange" and "accumulation on a world scale," in which value is
systematically transferred from periphery to core through international
trade at prices that do not reflect underlying labor values, entrenching
peripheral "extraversion" and blocked autocentric development.

This current provoked one of the sharpest intra-Marxist methodological
disputes of the late twentieth century. Robert Brenner's 1977 essay (here
verified via its 1982 anthology reprint) charged Wallerstein and Frank with
"neo-Smithian Marxism": by locating the origin and reproduction of
capitalism in exchange relations and the world market rather than in
historically specific relations of production and class struggle, they
allegedly collapse into the same circulationist logic as Adam Smith,
substituting a generic "commercialization" story for Marx's analysis of
capitalist social-property relations. This charge (elaborated further by
Political Marxism, covered in a separate domain of this review) forced
world-systems and dependency theorists to clarify whether "exploitation"
and "class" apply at the level of the world-system as a whole (a zone of
producers versus a zone of appropriators) or must remain anchored in
concrete relations of production within a bounded social formation — the
same fault line that runs through the transition-to-capitalism debates.
Weeks and Dore's Marxist critique of unequal-exchange theory anticipates
Brenner's charge from within the dependency tradition itself, while later
reassessments (Kvangraven, Song, Féliz) revisit the dispute, arguing that
Brenner's "trade versus production" dichotomy obscures the ways dependency
and world-systems theorists did theorize production relations, only at a
different (global) scale of analysis.

Recent scholarship (Chian, Geneciran, de Oliveira and Kvangraven, Pradella)
recovers the anti-colonial and non-Eurocentric stakes of this current,
tracing Wallerstein's turn to Marxism through his engagement with African
decolonization, situating dependency theory's 1972 Dakar conference
(organized by Amin) as a suppressed origin-point of a Global South
political economy, and reconnecting Marx's own writings on colonialism to
this world-scale framework. This positions world-systems/dependency theory
not merely as a sociological variant of Marxism but as the current that
most explicitly forces historical materialism to reckon with its own
Eurocentric starting assumptions.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the review's paradigm case of historical materialism's
application "beyond Europe" and beyond the single social formation, and it
supplies the clearest documented instance of an intra-Marxist
methodological schism (circulationism/"neo-Smithian Marxism" vs.
production-centered class analysis) that parallels and intersects with the
transition-to-capitalism debates treated in the Political Marxism domain.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Primary theoretical statements by Wallerstein (beyond the 2004 introductory
volume) and Frank's original 1966 "The Development of Underdevelopment"
essay were not independently recoverable with verified bibliographic
metadata through the available search tools within this session; secondary
literature discussing them was used instead. OpenAlex access was
intermittently rate-limited during this session, constraining the breadth
of that source relative to Semantic Scholar and citation chaining. Three
entries (wallerstein2004worldsystems, amin1976accumulation,
amin2010lawworldwide) are marked INCOMPLETE: no verified abstract could be
located for the two Amin books or the Wallerstein book through CORE,
Semantic Scholar, or OpenAlex; an NDPR match initially returned for Amin's
"The Law of Worldwide Value" was for an unrelated book (Postema's Law's
Rule) and was discarded rather than retained, so this entry is INCOMPLETE
rather than carrying a mismatched abstract.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Pair Brenner's neo-Smithian-Marxism charge directly against Kvangraven's,
Song's, and Féliz's reassessments to stage the live methodological dispute;
use Chian, Geneciran, and de Oliveira/Kvangraven to develop the
Eurocentrism/decolonial dimension as a distinct axis of evaluation from the
"trade vs. production" axis, since the two debates (methodological rigor
vs. geographic/epistemic scope) are often conflated in secondary
commentary.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- World-systems/dependency (circulationist, per Brenner's critics): 4 papers
  (Wallerstein 2004, Amin 1976, Amin 2010, Petras 1981) — capitalism and
  exploitation are properties of the world division of labor as a whole,
  mediated through unequal exchange.
- Political-Marxist/production-centered critique: 1 primary paper (Brenner
  1982) plus its direct reception (Denemark and Thomas 1988) — capitalism
  is a historically specific set of class/social-property relations, and
  "development of underdevelopment" mistakes market dependence for
  capitalist exploitation.
- Reassessment/synthesis positions: 4 papers (Kvangraven 2020, Song 2015,
  Féliz 2021, Weeks and Dore 1979) — arguing the trade/production dichotomy
  is overdrawn and that dependency theory has its own (underappreciated)
  production-centered variants.
- Decolonial/intellectual-historical recovery: 5 papers (Chian 2025,
  Geneciran 2024, de Oliveira and Kvangraven 2023, Pradella 2017, Stephens
  2016, Kay 2011) — situating the school's non-Eurocentric ambitions and
  internal intellectual development.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Contemporary Defenses, Revivals, and Ecological Extensions
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-11
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 7, Medium: 6, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef (verify_paper.py)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain closes the review by tracking historical materialism's afterlife
once the political conditions that once seemed to guarantee its truth
(existing "actually existing socialism," a industrially ascendant labor
movement) had collapsed or receded. Two developments dominate. First,
ecological Marxism reopens historical materialism's account of the
human-nature "metabolism," treating the theory's supposed silence on
ecological limits as an artifact of Cold War-era Marxism-Leninism and
productivist Second International readings rather than of Marx's own texts.
John Bellamy Foster's "metabolic rift" thesis (1999) is the pivot: reading
Marx's late engagement with Justus von Liebig's soil-chemistry critique of
capitalist agriculture, Foster argues Marx diagnosed capitalism as
systematically severing "the metabolic interaction between man and the
earth," a rupture in natural nutrient cycling that anticipates today's
planetary ecological crisis. Paul Burkett's contemporaneous work grounds
the same claim in Marx's value theory, showing nature's role as
"free gift" and material substratum of value-production is not incidental
but constitutive of Marx's critique of political economy. Kohei Saito
extends this reading philologically, mining Marx's unpublished natural-
science notebooks (published in the ongoing Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe,
MEGA2) to argue Marx's ecological thought deepened rather than receded
after Capital, culminating (per Saito) in a late turn toward "degrowth
communism" that abandons the productive-forces-development narrative
long attributed to orthodox historical materialism. This is not merely
exegetical: it directly revises the "primacy thesis" that Cohen's
analytical reconstruction placed at the center of historical materialism,
suggesting Marx himself came to doubt that expanding productive forces
is either possible or desirable without ecological limit.

The metabolic-rift program has itself become internally contested,
supplying the domain's clearest "live" theoretical dispute. Jason W.
Moore's "world-ecology" framework charges metabolic-rift thinking with
reproducing a Cartesian "Nature/Society" dualism that separates an
external "nature" acted upon by an internal "society," proposing instead
that capitalism should be theorized as "capitalism-in-nature," a single
"world-ecology" in which value relations and biophysical processes are
co-produced. Andreas Malm's rejoinder ("Against Hybridism," 2019) defends
metabolic rift's dualism as a considered "property dualism" -- nature and
society are equally material but really distinct kinds of relations -- and
argues Moore's hybridism, traced to Bruno Latour, dissolves the very
distinction needed to identify capitalism's responsibility for ecological
harm. Gellert's chapter surveys this and the adjacent "ecologically unequal
exchange" tradition, asking whether the three frameworks name substantively
different mechanisms or merely redescribe the same phenomena in rival
vocabularies. Foster and Burkett's own "anti-critique" defends the
metabolic-rift program against a decade of criticism (including from
Moore and others) that it lapses into a naturalistic determinism at odds
with Marx's dialectical method.

Second, this domain covers defenses of historical materialism as a general
theory of history and social change conducted under post-1989 conditions,
where the theory could no longer draw legitimacy from an existing
socialist state or a rising labor movement. Vivek Chibber's "What Is
Living and What Is Dead in the Marxist Theory of History" (2011,
Historical Materialism) directly engages the wreckage of the primacy
thesis left by attempts (by Erik Olin Wright and colleagues, and by Alan
Carling) to rescue G.A. Cohen's determinist theory of history, arguing
that these rescue attempts are largely unsuccessful and, to the extent
they succeed, do so only by converting historical materialism into a
class-struggle theory -- which Chibber takes as the demise of the
classical, technological-determinist version of the theory, though not as
a lapse into pure voluntarism. Chibber's "Postcolonial Theory and the
Specter of Capital" (2013) mounts a parallel, more polemical defense:
against postcolonial and Subaltern Studies claims that capital's
"universalizing tendencies" are a Eurocentric fiction, Chibber argues
historical materialism's core categories (class formation, capitalist
competition, structural compulsion) do generalize across colonial and
postcolonial social formations, provoking a sustained and still-unsettled
controversy (represented here by Lazarus's critical response, which
credits Chibber's defense of capital's universalizing tendency while
arguing he conflates Subaltern Studies with postcolonial theory more
broadly) about whether Chibber's defense rehabilitates or flattens the
insights of Subaltern Studies. Finally, the introduction to the 2019
"Oxford Handbook of Karl Marx" (Prew, Rotta, Smith, and Vidal) offers this
domain's synthetic retrospective, arguing across sociology, economics,
political science, philosophy, geography, and ecology that Marx's
"foundational" categories -- historical materialism among them -- continue
to be "defended, elaborated and applied" to twenty-first-century social
science rather than superseded.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain demonstrates that historical materialism did not end with the
Cohen/analytical-Marxism debates or the fall of Marxism-Leninism but
migrated into new terrain -- ecological crisis and postcolonial/global
capitalism -- where its core commitments (productive forces and relations
of production, base/superstructure, the primacy of material life-process)
are being simultaneously revised, defended, and contested. It gives the
review a genuinely open, contemporary endpoint rather than closing the
narrative in the twentieth century.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
OpenAlex access was persistently rate-limited for most of this session
(repeated queries returned "partial" with zero results despite retries at
staggered intervals), constraining cross-verification of some entries to
Semantic Scholar, CORE, PhilPapers, and CrossRef; it recovered sufficiently
during Stage 5.5 abstract enrichment to supply several abstracts below.
Saito's 2024 English trade edition ("Slow Down: How Degrowth Communism Can
Save the Earth," cited in the SEP "Karl Marx" bibliography) was
deliberately excluded as duplicative of the argument already covered by
the academic monograph "Marx in the Anthropocene" (2023, Cambridge
University Press) included here. No dedicated SEP or IEP entry exists for
"ecological Marxism" or "metabolic rift"; encyclopedia coverage of this
domain is limited to the "Karl Marx" SEP entry's brief discussion of
Saito's "eco-socialist" and "degrowth communism" readings under
"Alternative Interpretations." Four entries (burkett1999marxnature,
saito2017ecosocialism, gellert2018distinctions, malm2016fossilcapital,
chibber2013postcolonial) are marked INCOMPLETE: no verified abstract could
be located through Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, or CORE. During automated
NDPR fallback lookup, the script initially attached an abstract to
burkett1999marxnature from an unrelated NDPR review (on a contemporary
metaphysics-of-powers book); this mismatch was caught during review and
the erroneous abstract was removed, leaving the entry correctly marked
INCOMPLETE rather than populated with fabricated content.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Frame this domain as the review's live-debate endpoint: (1) the
metabolic-rift/world-ecology dispute (Foster/Burkett/Saito vs. Moore, with
Malm and Gellert as adjudicators) as a direct continuation of the
base/superstructure and technological-determinism debates found in the
Cohen/analytical-Marxism and Political Marxism domains, now conducted over
nature rather than class; and (2) the Chibber/postcolonial-theory dispute
as a continuation of the Eurocentrism critique found in the
world-systems/dependency domain, now conducted over historical
materialism's claim to theorize a single, universalizing capitalist
world-history.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Metabolic rift (nature/society as really distinct but equally material):
  6 papers (Foster 1999, Burkett 1999, Saito 2017, Saito 2023, Foster and
  Burkett 2016, Malm 2019) -- capitalism ruptures a determinate metabolic
  interchange between humanity and nature that Marx's mature political
  economy already theorized.
- World-ecology / hybridist critique: 1 paper (Moore 2017) -- rift thinking
  smuggles in a Cartesian dualism; capitalism must be theorized as a
  "world-ecology" internal to nature, not acting upon it from outside.
- Synthesis/adjudication of the rift-vs-ecology dispute: 1 paper (Gellert
  2018) -- compares metabolic rift, world-ecology, and ecologically
  unequal exchange as partially overlapping, partially rival frameworks.
- Defense/reconstruction of historical materialism as a general theory of
  history and social change: 3 papers (Chibber 2011, Chibber 2013, Prew
  et al. 2018) -- historical materialism's core categories remain
  defensible and empirically productive after the collapse of both
  technological-determinist orthodoxy and Marxism-Leninist state power.
- Critical reception of the post-1990s defenses: 1 paper (Lazarus 2016) --
  contests Chibber's universalizing defense of capital's categories against
  postcolonial theory.
====================================================================
}

