    title = "Problems of The German Ideology",
    year = "2018",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Catalogues specific textual and conceptual problems in treating The German Ideology's "Feuerbach chapter" as a finished statement of historical materialism, including its incomplete argumentation about the relation between the division of labour, forms of property, and the emergence of class society -- problems obscured when later expositions (including Marx's own 1859 Preface) present the theory in more compressed and apparently settled form. RELEVANCE: Complements Carver and Blank's manuscript-based case by showing that even setting aside editorial history, the content of the German Ideology material is less determinate on core categories (forces/relations of production, division of labour) than the 1859 Preface's condensed formulation suggests -- direct evidence for the domain's question about tensions between texts. POSITION: Internal conceptual critique of the German Ideology as a source-text for historical materialism, sympathetic to treating the theory as underdetermined by Marx's own writings rather than as a settled doctrine.},
    keywords = "german-ideology, division-of-labour, textual-problems, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "This chapter considers the significant implications of recent work by Terrell Carver and Daniel Blank that argues powerfully that Marx and Engels never produced anything like a coherent manuscript that could be published in the form of a book called The German Ideology . What was published in that form by David Ryazanov in 1921 was a pastiche of fragmented writings put together on the basis of his own conviction that he could discern in them an account of Marx and Engels’s method of historical analysis. It is, however, striking to what extent this supposed work of theirs incorporates ideas taken directly and uncritically from liberal historians and economists. When it is recognized that the point of the core passages was a specifically political polemic against German philosophers, as Carver and Blank argue, it can be seen that the point of Marx and Engels was to contrast the vacuous and idealist politics of these supposedly radical philosophers with the profoundly concrete and practical considerations evident in the work of mainstream liberals in France and Britain. Far from a statement of Marx and Engels’s method, these passages made clear how “backward” the philosophers were in comparison even to liberals. These keywords were added by machine and not by the authors. This process is experimental and the keywords may be updated as the learning algorithm improves.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{mclellan2006life,
    author = "McLellan, David",
    title = "Karl Marx: His Life and Thought",
    year = "2006",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: A comprehensive intellectual biography tracing Marx's development from his doctoral studies in ancient philosophy through the 1844-46 manuscripts, the Grundrisse, and Capital, situating the formulation of historical materialism within Marx's shifting personal, political, and editorial circumstances (including the non-publication of the German Ideology manuscripts and the late recovery of the Grundrisse). RELEVANCE: Supplies biographical-chronological grounding for the domain's question about shifts between texts -- showing, for instance, how the 1857-58 composition of the Grundrisse and the compressed 1859 Preface emerged from the same short period of intensive economic study, complicating any simple "early vs. late" periodization. POSITION: Standard biographical-contextual scholarship in the McLellan/Carver tradition of source-critical Marx studies, first published 1973 and repeatedly revised as new archival material (MEGA volumes) became available.},
    keywords = "marx-biography, textual-chronology, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{musto2015youngmarxmyth,
    author = "Musto, Marcello",
    title = "The 'Young Marx' Myth in Interpretations of the Economic-Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844",
    year = "2015",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the reception history of the 1844 Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts (only published in full in 1932) and argues that the widespread narrative of a "young," humanist-alienation Marx superseded by a "mature," scientific-materialist Marx of the German Ideology and later texts is a retrospective construction shaped by the order and timing in which Marx's manuscripts were actually published, rather than a break internal to Marx's own thought. RELEVANCE: Directly targets the domain's question about "young vs. mature Marx" scholarship and its bearing on the canonical status of the 1859 Preface; shows that debates over continuity/discontinuity in historical materialism are inseparable from the archival accident of which manuscripts became available when. POSITION: Reception-historical/philological argument against sharp periodization (contra Althusserian "epistemological break" readings); aligns with the "continuity" side of the debate Cowling (2023) surveys.},
    keywords = "young-marx, mature-marx, 1844-manuscripts, reception-history, philology, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Focusing on the dissemination and reception history of the Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844, this article will critically examine the famous controversy surrounding the relationship between Marx’s ‘early’ and ‘mature’ writings. A review of all the major books published worldwide (especially in Germany, France, the Soviet Union and English-speaking countries) on Marx’s early writings is followed by a plea for a new and rigorous reading of Marx’s Paris manuscripts, which have been wrongly considered by almost all interpreters as a finished work. Careful textual analysis of the Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844 alongside the so-called Paris notebooks makes it possible to refute conceptions of the former as a fully fledged text either prefiguring Marx’s thought as a whole (as Landshut or the French existentialists argued) or advancing a well-defined theory opposed to that of Marx’s ‘scientific’ maturity (as Marxist–Leninist orthodoxy or Althusser claimed).",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{musto2008grundrisse,
    author = "Musto, Marcello",
    title = "Karl Marx's Grundrisse: Foundations of the Critique of Political Economy 150 Years Later",
    year = "2008",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: An edited volume of essays (with contributions on the Grundrisse's composition, its relation to Hegel's Logic, and its place in Marx's oeuvre) documenting that the Grundrisse -- composed in 1857-58, first published in German only in 1939/1953, and not translated into English until 1973 -- was for most of the twentieth century unavailable to the readers who nonetheless treated the 1859 Preface as the canonical statement of historical materialism. RELEVANCE: Provides the philological basis for the domain's contrast between the "compressed, seemingly deterministic 1859 Preface" and the "more open-ended, dialectical Grundrisse": the two texts were written within roughly a year of each other but had radically different publication and reception histories, which is essential context for explaining why the Preface rather than the Grundrisse became canonical. POSITION: Historical-philological scholarship on the Grundrisse's textual history and belated reception, rather than a single interpretive thesis about the theory's content.},
    keywords = "grundrisse, textual-history, reception-history, philology, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc, METADATA\_CLEANED: doi",
    abstract = "Having abandoned the Grundrisse in May 1858 to make room for work on A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, Marx used parts of it in composing this latter text but then almost never drew on it again. In fact, although it was his habit to invoke his own previous studies, even to transcribe whole passages from them, none of the preparatory manuscripts for Capital, with the exception of those of 1861-3, contains any reference to the Grundrisse. It lay among all the other drafts that he had no intention of bringing into service as he became absorbed in solving more specific problems than they had addressed.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{satz1989marxism,
    author = "Satz, Debra",
    title = "Marxism, Materialism and Historical Progress",
    year = "1989",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Marx offers two distinct and never explicitly reconciled explanations of historical change: in The German Ideology, the growth of human productive powers is the fundamental cause, with social forms changing to accommodate further productive development; in the Communist Manifesto and the Grundrisse, by contrast, it is the desires and interests of classes -- class struggle aimed at ending specific conditions of oppression -- that determine both when an old social form is replaced and what replaces it. Marx never specifies whether these are one mechanism or two. RELEVANCE: Provides direct textual evidence, drawn from close reading of the primary texts named in this domain, for the tension the domain's key questions ask about: a productive-forces-driven account (German Ideology, echoed in the compressed 1859 Preface) sitting alongside a class-struggle-driven account (Manifesto, Grundrisse). POSITION: Textual/analytic argument for an unresolved dualism internal to Marx's own formulations, prior to and independent of any later interpretive school's gloss (e.g., Cohen's productive-forces-primacy reconstruction).",
    keywords = "german-ideology, communist-manifesto, grundrisse, class-struggle, productive-forces, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: volume, pages, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "The theory of historical materialism is the core commitment of Marx’s social theory. More than his views on markets, philosophical methods, the state and social institutions, it is this theory which sets Marx’s views apart from alternative traditions in political philosophy. Marx believes that there is a tendency for societies to make moral and material progress. The point of Marx’s theory of historical materialism is to offer a theory of the mechanisms which produce this tendency. However, in Marx’s own formulation, the precise nature of these mechanisms remains obscure. In The German Ideology, Marx emphasizes the growth of human productive powers as the fundamental cause of historical change and progress: social forms (e.g., feudalism, capitalism) change in order to adapt to the requirements of further productive development. By contrast, in The Communist Manifesto and the Grundrisse, Marx emphasizes the desires and interests of classes as fundamental to explaining social change. Here, it is class struggles (aimed at ending specific conditions of oppression) which determine not only when an old social form will be replaced by a new one, but also the nature of the new social form itself. Marx never specifies the connection between these two explanations of historical change, between the development of human productive powers and class struggles. In particular, Marx is not explicit as to whether there are two distinct mechanisms at work in the production of historical progress, or only one.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{fracchia2004transhistorical,
    author = "Fracchia, Joseph",
    title = "On Transhistorical Abstractions and the Intersection of Historical Theory and Social Critique",
    year = "2004",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Engages Moishe Postone's claim that the Grundrisse (1857-58), not The German Ideology (1845), should be regarded as Marx's first genuinely "mature" work, against Marx's own 1859 retrospective self-assessment pointing to the German Ideology as his moment of theoretical "self-clarification." Uses this disagreement to examine how the Grundrisse treats categories like "labour" and "production in general" as historically specific abstractions rather than transhistorical constants, in contrast to the more fixed categorial apparatus of the 1859 Preface. RELEVANCE: Speaks directly to the domain's question about shifts between the "compressed, seemingly deterministic 1859 Preface" and the "more open-ended, dialectical Grundrisse," and shows that even the question of which text counts as Marx's mature statement is itself contested in the secondary literature. POSITION: Critical-theoretical (Frankfurt-School-adjacent, via Postone) argument for the Grundrisse's methodological superiority over both the German Ideology and the compressed Preface as a guide to Marx's mature method.},
    keywords = "grundrisse, german-ideology, 1859-preface, postone, historical-materialism, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Historical Materialism , volume 12:3 (125–146) © Koninklijke Brill NV, Leiden, 2004 Also available online – www.brill.nl 1 Marx 1981, p. 10. Joseph Fracchia On Transhistorical Abstractions and the Intersection of Historical Theory and Social Critique Reading Marx as a critical social theorist, Moishe Postone designates the Grundrisse (1857–8) as the first of Marx’s ‘mature’ writings. In this, he disagrees with Marx’s own retrospective, written in 1859, which points to the first sketch of the materialist conception of history in the German Ideology (1845) as his work of self-clarification and, presumably, his entry into maturity. 1 My point in raising",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{sayers1984marxism,
    author = "Sayers, Sean",
    title = "Marxism and the Dialectical Method: A Critique of G.A. Cohen",
    year = "2013",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that G.A. Cohen's reconstruction of historical materialism (Development Thesis, Primacy Thesis, functional explanation) rests on a mechanical, non-dialectical reading that abstracts the 1859 Preface's formulations from Marx's Hegelian method, and that this abstraction produces the very coherence problems (regarding functional explanation and historical regression) that occupy so much of the analytic literature responding to Cohen. RELEVANCE: Represents the principal dialectical counter-reading to the Cohen-style nomological interpretation of the 1859 Preface, directly engaging the domain's question about the role of Hegelian dialectic in shaping how the canonical statement is read. POSITION: Dialectical/Hegelian-Marxist critique of analytic (Cohen-style) historical materialism; foundational reference point for subsequent "dialectic vs. mechanism" debates over the Preface's status.},
    keywords = "dialectic, hegel, cohen-critique, 1859-preface, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "11 Humanism=Speciesism? - 1 - Marx on humans and animals",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{ruben1981cohen,
  author = {Ruben, David-Hillel},
  title = {Cohen, Marx and the Primacy Thesis},
  journal = {British Journal of Political Science},
  year = {1981},
  volume = {11},
  number = {2},
  pages = {227--234},
  doi = {10.1017/s0007123400002581},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Credits Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of History with setting
  "a new standard for Marxist philosophy" through its precision and
  sustained attention to argument, situating this achievement against a
  continental Marxist tradition Ruben regards as marred by an absence of
  careful definition and rigorous argumentation; on this basis subjects
  Cohen's primacy thesis to close philosophical scrutiny.

  RELEVANCE: An early and influential philosophical assessment weighing
  whether the primacy thesis survives analytical scrutiny, directly bearing
  on this domain's central evaluative question about the thesis's staying
  power.

  POSITION: Sympathetic but critical engagement testing the primacy thesis's
  coherence from within the analytical tradition Cohen inaugurated.
  },
  keywords = {primacy-thesis, analytical-marxism, Medium},
  abstract = {As Jon Elster remarked in an earlier review, G. A. Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence ‘sets a new standard for Marxist philosophy’. In one way, the praise is too faint. Existing paradigms of continental Marxist philosophy are marred by an absence of careful definition, clear and precise argumentation, and the sort of sustained discussion that is committed to the teasing out of the intellectual difficulties of the author's own position – in short, marred by an absence of those virtues in the attainment of which the philosophy practised in Britain, the United States, and the Scandinavian countries on the whole excels. Cohen would, presumably, agree that this has nothing intrinsically to do with Marx, or Marxism, but in the main arises from the fortuitous coupling of Marxism with continental philosophical fashions. If this sounds like some sort of cultural chauvinism, it also happens to be the truth. So the ‘new’ standards for Marxist philosophy, then, are only the same standards by which it is right to judge all philosophy, wherever it is written, and which any philosophy must pass in order to be accounted good. What is perhaps less faint praise of Cohen is to say that his book is good philosophy (without the qualifying adjective ‘Marxist’). It is an ingenious and imaginative volume, in which precision and detailed attention to argument count for everything.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@misc{mcbride1982tendencies,
    author = "McBride, William L.",
    title = "Tendencies in Marxology and Tendencies in History",
    year = "1982",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: A review essay jointly assessing G.A. Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence, Michel Henry's two-volume Marx, John McMurtry's The Structure of Marx's World-View, and Melvin Rader's Marx's Interpretation of History, comparing how each reconstructs the relation between the 1859 Preface's compressed claims and the fuller textual record (German Ideology, Grundrisse, Capital), and noting how differently each author weighs determinism against human agency in Marx's account of historical change. RELEVANCE: Offers a contemporaneous, comparative map of the major early-1980s book-length reconstructions of historical materialism, showing the range of positions -- from Cohen's near-deterministic reading to more agency-centred alternatives -- that later scholarship (including the domain's other entries) responds to. POSITION: Synoptic review rather than an independent interpretive thesis; useful chiefly for locating Cohen's reconstruction relative to its immediate rivals.",
    keywords = "cohen-reconstruction, review-essay, determinism, agency, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{cowling2023strategies,
    author = "Cowling, Mark",
    title = "Strategies of continuity and discontinuity in the interpretation of Marx's work: The case for discontinuity",
    year = "2023",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Surveys the interpretive strategies scholars have used to handle apparent shifts across Marx's career -- from the 1844 Manuscripts through the German Ideology to the 1859 Preface and the Grundrisse -- distinguishing "continuity" readings (which treat the corpus as expressing a single evolving but consistent theory) from "discontinuity" readings (which posit real breaks, echoing but not identical to Althusser's "epistemological break"), and argues for a qualified discontinuity position. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the domain's question about tensions and shifts between texts by explicitly cataloguing the interpretive options available for handling the German Ideology/1859 Preface/Grundrisse relationship, providing an up-to-date (2023) map of an argument running throughout the domain's other entries (Musto, Fracchia, Satz). POSITION: Argues for discontinuity, against both orthodox continuity readings and Althusser's specific version of the break thesis; explicitly positions itself within the young-Marx/mature-Marx debate.},
    keywords = "young-marx, mature-marx, epistemological-break, continuity-discontinuity, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "The publication of Marx’s Economic and Philosophical Manuscripts (EPM) in German in 1933 immediately triggered off debate as to where the document fitted in Marx’s theories generally. The obvious possibilities were first, that these writings could be dismissed as juvenilia, and that Marxism as understood by figures as diverse as Lenin, Kautsky, Bernstein, Rosa Luxemburg and Trotsky, none of whom said anything significant about alienation, could be studied and argued about while disregarding the EPM. Second, there were actually numerous reappearances of the theory of alienation in the writings of Marx after 1847, and the alienation theory must be very important in his mature writings. In what follows, I will argue the case for the unfashionable first alternative, but also be reviewing many of the arguments for and against continuity in Marx’s work, hopefully making the article useful irrespective of which side one takes.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{marx1998germanideology,
    author = "Marx, Karl and Engels, Friedrich",
    title = "The German Ideology: Including Theses on Feuerbach and Introduction to The Critique of Political Economy",
    year = "1998",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: The primary source manuscripts (1845-46), co-written with Engels, in which Marx first sets out the premises later compressed into the 1859 Preface: that humans are essentially productive beings, that the satisfaction of needs generates new needs, that "forms of society arise corresponding to the state of development of human productive forces," and that material production determines social forms and consciousness rather than the reverse. Includes the Theses on Feuerbach and the fragmentary Introduction to the Critique of Political Economy. RELEVANCE: The primary textual source for the domain's questions about the earliest extended statement of the forces/relations-of-production framework, prior to its compression in the 1859 Preface; should be read alongside Carver and Blank's and Comninel's philological cautions about the text's editorial history. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "german-ideology, primary-source, forces-of-production, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {In recent years, there has been much more attention directed to the philosophical dimensions of German Romanticism, a movement that for far too long was dismissed as merely literary. The welcome result of this attention is the emergence of a richer dialogue that reveals the historical context of a movement whose leading figures included Friedrich von Hardenberg (Novalis), Friedrich Hölderlin, F.W.J. Schelling, F.D.E. Schleiermacher, and Friedrich Schlegel. We are, at long last, in a position to appreciate how much these thinkers have to offer to contemporary discussions ranging from questions of culture, art, and religion, to metaphysics, epistemology, and the natural sciences. Leif Weatherby's excellent study is a welcome contribution to the growing scholarship on the philosophical dimensions of German Romanticism, and his careful, creative work, which blends literary, philosophical, and historical analysis, will surely awaken even more interest in the field. The volume is part of theForms of LivingSeries, edited by Stefanos Geroulanos and Todd Meyers, which in turn is part of theModern Language Initiativeat Fordham University Press. The editors claim that the series, "seeks to provide an outlet for theoretically and methodologically rigorous writing theorized and articulated through various disciplines, frames, and attempts" and that it "begins new conversations on what is at stake betweenknowledgeandlifein the forms that each takes." abstract\\_source = {ndpr}, In the three parts of Weatherby's impressive volume, which covers figures as diverse as Leibniz, Blumenbach, Kant, Herder, Hölderlin, Schelling, Novalis, and Goethe, he delivers on the promise of the series to open a new conversation about what is at stake in discussions of knowledge and life. Weatherby focuses upon the term 'organ' and uses that term to conduct a discussion centered upon life and metaphysical processes from Kant to Marx, with special emphasis on the work of the German Romantics. As he tells us early in the book, organs "take over the function of mediators between body and soul" and so "come in Romantic discourse to define being as such" (p. 2). Weatherby's skill as a reader of Romanticism is well-reflected in the work he does to defend what I take to be the most promising and innovative claim of the book, namely, that The layer of Romantic discourse centering on the word 'organ' reveals a different Romanticism -- one focused on Critical methodology, on a new metaphysical approach to concrete scientific and political topics. The doctrine that emerges -- reconstructed here from its incomplete textual expression -- can be called 'organology' (p. 123). Weatherby, who characterizes Schelling as "the biggest risk-taker among the German Idealists" (p. 174), is also a risk-taker. It is no timid task to reveal a new Romanticism, via a reconstruction from "incomplete textual expression." To do so via organology, described by Weatherby as a new metaphysical approach to concrete scientificandpolitical topics, is not a task for the meek, but he does not shy away. The book opens with an overview of how the term 'organ' was understood during the 17thcentury, and how a change in its meaning signaled a shift in nothing less than the notion of life itself. Weatherby locates the heart of this shift in the contributions of the Romantics, writing that, "Romantic organology was meant to be a powerful intellectual tool for the refashioning of the natural and social worlds" (p. 10). An investigation of the relation of the natural and social worlds takes Weatherby on a long and winding road through the history of ideas (and, as he emphasizes, his is a tale of intellectual, literary, scientific, and philosophical history) from Leibniz to Marx. The scope reveals his ambition, and without focus and care, such ambition could easily lead to hazy arguments and confusing jargon. When I first saw the title of Weatherby's volume, I was apprehensive about its scope. Kant taught me long ago that "thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind" (CPR, B75/A51). Painful reading experience has taught me time and again that ambition without discipline is blind, leading to works that become a string of thoughts without useful content. I am happy to report that Weatherby's ambition is matched by his discipline as a scholar: he is a clear and careful writer, a reasonable risk-taker, finding the Aristotelian mean between rashness and cowardice. His book is a fine example of intellectual bravery. His ambition is also tempered by a humility that slows him just where it should and which results in a compelling case for the claims he defends. The result is a study that illuminates and also opens the field of Romanticism in innovative ways. In three parts: I) "Toward Organology," II) "Romantic Organology: Toward a Technological Metaphysics of Judgment," and III) "After Organology," Weatherby clarifies his terms and carefully supports his claims. The first three chapters set out a rich historical context for the problem of organology. Organology, as he goes on to argue, is a "metaphysical approach to concrete scientific and political topics" (p. 123), an approach that tells the "intertwining histories of nature-human cognition as a single theory" (p. 124). He presents the metaphysical issues related to organs and the emergence of life through a compelling discussion of the work of Leibniz, Blumenbach, Kant, and Herder. Having presented a detailed view of the organ of the soul, he then moves to a discussion of romantic organology in the four chapters that constitute the second part of his study. In his discussion of the Romantics, the works of Hölderlin, Schelling, and Novalis are highlighted and carefully analyzed. Chapter 7, "Between Myth and Science: Naturphilosophie and the Ends of Organology," is of great merit, as Weatherby here connects science and myth and presents a case for the connection betweenNaturphilosophieand organology, thereby shedding much needed light on what precisely thinkers of the period were after in their pursuit ofNaturphilosophie, which is more than simply a philosophy of nature. In Part III, "After Organology",the final two chapters of his book, he connects the work of Hegel and Goethe and then draws our attention to contemporary connections between organology and a Marxist social critique. In what follows, I will focus on what I take to be the heart of the book, which is Part II, "Romantic Organology: Toward a Technological Metaphysics of Judgment." At the beginning Weatherby maps his plan for the following four chapters. He frames his account of Romantic organology with the work of Hölderlin, Schelling, and Novalis. He begins Part II with the following account of the story that will unfold: To tell the intertwining histories of nature and human cognition as a single story was the common project of Novalis, Schlegel, and the scientists who wrote what they calledNaturphilosophie, the philosophy of nature. These "histories" sought to confer necessity upon contingent structures, not as a way of demonstrating their internal rationality, but as a way of gaining a theoretical toehold for intervening in these histories, for reproducing them as provisionally rational, in order to produce something radically new -- a different order of things. "Organs," then, were the locus of this philosophical hope, which combined methodology with application to the "real." Rather than producing a representation of a world, a Nature, or a history, the Romantics' focus on organs was meant to recast any possible sense of "world," "nature," or "being" (pp. 124-25).}
}

@misc{marx1996later,
    author = "Marx, Karl",
    editor = "Carver, Terrell",
    title = "Marx: Later Political Writings",
    year = "1996",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: A scholarly edition, with introduction and notes by Terrell Carver, collecting Marx's major post-1848 political writings in a single volume, including the Manifesto of the Communist Party, the 1859 "Preface" to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, and the "Introduction" to the Grundrisse -- the three texts whose relation is central to this domain's second key question. RELEVANCE: Provides a single, scholarly-edited primary-source anchor for comparing the Manifesto's and Grundrisse Introduction's treatment of class struggle and method against the compressed formulations of the 1859 Preface, edited by one of the domain's leading philological commentators (Carver). POSITION: Primary source, scholarly edition.},
    keywords = "1859-preface, communist-manifesto, grundrisse-introduction, primary-source, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Marx: Later Political Writings, first published in 1996, brings together translations of Marx's most important texts in political philosophy written after 1848. Marx challenged political theory to its very fundamentals, as his works do not follow traditional models for exploring politics theoretically. In his introduction, Terrell Carver situates Marx in a politics of democratic constitutionalism and revolutionary communism. The works are presented here complete, according to the first editions or the earliest manuscript state, and include the Manifesto of the Communist Party, the Preface of 1859 to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, The Civil War in France, and the little-known Notes on Adolph Wagner. More than most political theorists, Marx made contemporary politics the focus for his theoretical work. He created a distinctive kind of political theory, and this volume makes it accessible today.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{engels1962antiduhring,
    author = "Engels, Friedrich",
    title = "Anti-Dühring: Herr Eugen Dühring's Revolution in Science",
    year = "1962",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Engels's 1878 polemic against Eugen Dühring's rival system of socialism is, in Hal Draper's often-cited phrase quoted by later scholarship (see Blackledge 2017 below), "the only more or less systematic presentation of Marxism" produced by either Marx or Engels in their lifetimes; it lays out materialist dialectics, the critique of political economy, and a philosophy of history intended as a unified "world outlook." RELEVANCE: This is the primary anchor text for the domain's central question: because Anti-Dühring is the closest thing to a systematic exposition of "Marxism" endorsed (if not authored solely) by Marx himself, debates over whether it faithfully represents Marx's own historical materialism or already constitutes an "Engelsian" codification are foundational to assessing the economic-determinism charge. POSITION: Primary codifying text of orthodox/scientific Marxism, contested between continuity readings (Blackledge, Hollander) and divergence readings (Byron).},
    keywords = "Engels, Anti-Duhring, primary-source, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{blackledge2025crimea,
    author = "Blackledge, Paul",
    title = "Friedrich Engels, historical materialism and the Crimean War",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reading Engels's military journalism on the Crimean War, Blackledge argues these encyclopedia articles display a sophisticated, non-mechanical historical materialism rather than the "mechanical materialism and economic reductionism" for which Engels is often criticized; military organization is analyzed through an interplay of technology, social relations, and contingent political struggle. RELEVANCE: Directly tests the economic-determinism charge against Engels using material outside the canonical 1890s letters, offering an empirical case for continuity between Engels's practice and a non-reductive reading of historical materialism. POSITION: Rehabilitative/continuity reading of Engels against the "vulgar Marxist" caricature.},
    keywords = "Engels, historical-materialism, economic-determinism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{blackledge2017antiduhring,
    author = "Blackledge, Paul",
    title = "Practical Materialism: Engels's Anti-Dühring as Marxist Philosophy",
    year = "2017",
    abstract = "Frederick Engels's Anti-Dühring was the most important theoretical response to the emerging reformist tendencies within European socialism in the 19th century. It also proved to be Engels's most influential, and controversial work. Because it is, as Hal Draper points out, 'the only more or less systematic presentation of Marxism' by either by Marx or Engels, anyone wanting to reinterpret Marx must first detach it from his seal of approval. It is thus around Anti-Dühring and related texts that debates about the relationship of Marx to 'Engelsian' Marxism have tended to focus. This essay re-engages these debates.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge situates Anti-Dühring as Engels's direct theoretical rebuttal of the reformist tendencies that would later crystallize as Bernsteinian revisionism, and argues that reinterpretations of Marx which downplay or reject Engels must first contend with the fact that Anti-Dühring carried Marx's own "seal of approval." RELEVANCE: Central to evaluating whether the "economic determinism" charge is properly aimed at Engels rather than Marx: Blackledge's account complicates any clean separation of the two, since Marx endorsed the text scholars use to indict Engels. POSITION: Continuity/rehabilitative reading; treats Anti-Dühring as authoritative "Marxist philosophy," not a distortion of Marx.},
    keywords = "Engels, Anti-Duhring, Marxist-philosophy, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{blackledge2006kautsky,
    author = "Blackledge, Paul",
    title = "Karl Kautsky and Marxist Historiography",
    year = "2006",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge argues the traditional dismissal of Kautsky's Marxism as "mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism" is a caricature; Kautsky's historical studies of the early Church, the Reformation, and contemporary Russian and American social formations (especially pre-1910) show a sophisticated and powerful theory of history, even though his Marxism suffered consistent political weaknesses. RELEVANCE: Provides the strongest secondary-literature case that Kautsky's "orthodox Marxism," at least in its earlier historiographical practice, does not straightforwardly deserve the "economic determinism" label frequently pinned on Second International orthodoxy. POSITION: Rehabilitative reading of Kautsky's historical method, distinguished from his later political/theoretical decline after 1910.},
    keywords = "Kautsky, historiography, Second-International, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "The traditional dismissive characterization of Karl Kautsky's Marxism as a form of mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism is a caricature that obscures the very real and important contribution he made to historical materialism in the early years of the last century. Kautsky's historical studies of both the early Church and Reformation “communism,” as well as his attempts to analyze the contemporary Russian and American social formations, show that, while Kautsky's Marxism suffered from consistent political weaknesses, his theory of history, especially as it was articulated in the years before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful. While the power of Kautsky's Marxism became muted after this period, the weaknesses of his later works should not be allowed to obscure the insights contained within his earlier work; insights that informed some of the best work of the next generation of Marxists.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{shandro2014kautsky,
    author = "Shandro, Alan",
    title = "On the Relation of Theory and Practice: Karl Kautsky and the First Post-Marxist",
    year = "2014",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Shandro argues it has become customary to trace the deficiencies of Kautsky's "orthodox Marxism" to a reified social ontology that leaves no room for political will, separating theory from practice; but close examination of Kautskyan texts within the political context of the SPD shows this interpretation does not withstand scrutiny and instead risks reproducing the real theoretical-political limitations of Kautskyan orthodoxy, which required a notion of the working class as "unity-in-diversity" rather than simple class polarization against Bernstein's revisionist challenge. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses whether "economic determinism" is the right diagnostic category for Kautsky's orthodoxy, proposing instead a more specific critique centered on the theory-practice relation and the party's response to social differentiation within the working class. POSITION: Critical-but-non-reductive reading of Kautskyan orthodoxy, framed against both the "reified ontology" caricature and Bernstein's revisionism.},
    keywords = "Kautsky, orthodox-Marxism, theory-practice, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "Kautsky's place as the great renegade in the history of Marxism and the workers' movement has been fixed by Lenin's repeated and virulent denunciations of him during the war and in the aftermath of the October Revolution. Kautsky's identification of Marxism - as the theoretical expression of the unity of the working-class movement - with the socialist consciousness of the proletariat serves to establish a concrete connection between theory and the political practice of the SPD's parliamentary project. The chapter presents Marxism not only as the union of socialism and the workers' movement but also as the synthesis of natural and social science; of French, German and English thought; and of theory and practice. Kautsky's perfunctory acknowledgement of the infinite progress of knowledge notwithstanding, Marxism is here related to the labour movement as the resolution of all contradictions.Keywords: Kautsky; Lenin; Marxism; October Revolution; socialism",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{lewis2017kautsky,
    author = "Lewis, Ben",
    title = "Marxism after Marx: Karl Kautsky's Disputed Legacy",
    year = "2017",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Lewis surveys the disputed legacy of Karl Kautsky, who today is mainly remembered for his polemics against the Bolshevik regime and as the "renegade" pilloried in Lenin's The Proletarian Revolution and the Renegade Kautsky, and introduces a special-issue reassessment of Kautsky's theoretical contribution independent of that later political rupture. RELEVANCE: Frames the historiographical stakes of rehabilitating or indicting Kautsky as "orthodox Marxism's" chief systematizer, situating the economic-determinism debate within the broader question of how Kautsky's reputation was shaped by the Bolshevik/Second-International split. POSITION: Historiographical framing piece introducing revisionist (in the scholarly, not political, sense) reassessment of Kautsky.},
    keywords = "Kautsky, Second-International, historiography, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Abstract Today, Karl Kautsky (1854–1938) is mainly remembered for his polemics against the young Bolshevik regime or as the ‘renegade’ in Lenin’sThe Proletarian Revolution and the Renegade Kautsky(1918), which pillories him for his wavering stance in opposing World War I and his (later) outright hostility to the Russian Revolution of October 1917. Kautsky’s authority as a Marxist theoretician was seriously called into question ever since Lenin’s polemic. During the Cold War in particular, a consensus emerged which suggested that Kautsky’s views of democracy, organisation and revolutionary change had little or nothing to do with the political practice of Russian Bolshevism and the Russian Revolution of 1917. Recently, however, several studies have challenged this consensus. They highlight the profound impact which Kautsky had on the development of Russian Bolshevism and make the case that – prior to his renegacy in 1914 – thinkers such as Lenin and Trotsky viewed Kautsky as the legitimate intellectual heir of Marx and Engels. This article introduces an autobiographical essay written by Kautsky in 1924 and calls for closer engagement with his œuvre as a whole.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{byron2022engelsianism,
    author = "Byron, Chris",
    title = "Engelsianism, Second Internationalism, and the Loss of Marx's Critical Method",
    year = "2022",
    abstract = "This essay argues that the predominant view of 'Marxism' was largely shaped by Engels and the Second International. The predominant view of Marxism includes a metaphysical theory (Dialectical Materialism) and a Scientific Theory (Historical Materialism). Marx never used either phrase. Moreover, a close reading of his mature works and letters reveals that he explicitly rejected the content of those theories. Hence, in the shaping of 'Marxism', a series of confusions and inaccuracies about history and economic development were attributed to Marx. One source of miscommunication was Engels, who served as an intermediary between Marx's thought and the Second International.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Byron argues that "Dialectical Materialism" and a law-governed, scientific "Historical Materialism" are terminological and doctrinal inventions of Engels and the Second International rather than Marx's own positions; Marx himself never used either phrase, and his mature works and letters explicitly reject the content of those theories, with Engels serving as the intermediary who transmitted a distorted "Marxism" to the Second International. RELEVANCE: This is the domain's sharpest statement of the divergence thesis and the clearest direct answer to the research question of whether "economic determinism" targets Engels/Kautsky/Plekhanov rather than Marx - Byron argues yes, unequivocally, and locates the mechanism of transmission in Engels specifically. POSITION: Strong divergence thesis: Engelsian/Second-International "Marxism" as a doctrinal departure from Marx's own critical method.},
    keywords = "Engels, Second-International, economic-determinism, Marx-Engels-divergence, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{steger1992bernstein,
    author = "Steger, Manfred",
    title = "Historical materialism and ethics: Eduard Bernstein's revisionist perspective",
    year = "1992",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Steger reconstructs Bernstein's revisionist critique of historical materialism as centrally an ethical intervention: Bernstein challenged the deterministic, "scientific" self-presentation of orthodox Marxism (associated with Kautsky) by arguing that socialism required an independent Kantian ethical foundation rather than a guaranteed outcome of economic law. RELEVANCE: Establishes the philosophical stakes of the Bernstein-Kautsky Revisionismusstreit for this domain: the dispute was explicitly about whether historical materialism should be presented as a determinist science (Kautsky's orthodoxy) or supplemented/replaced by ethical voluntarism (Bernstein), making it a direct precedent for later anti-determinist readings of Marxism. POSITION: Bernsteinian revisionism as ethically motivated rejection of orthodox economic determinism.},
    keywords = "Bernstein, revisionism, ethics, Second-International, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume"
}

@misc{rogers2015revisionist,
    author = "Rogers, H. Kendall",
    title = "Before the Revisionist Controversy: Kautsky, Bernstein, and the Meaning of Marxism, 1895--1898",
    year = "2015",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Rogers reconstructs the intellectual formation of the Bernstein-Kautsky dispute in the years immediately preceding its public eruption (1895-1898), tracing how both figures' understandings of "the meaning of Marxism" were already diverging before the Revisionismusstreit became an open party controversy. RELEVANCE: Supplies the detailed prehistory of the political-theoretical split this domain treats as central: it documents the process by which Kautsky's "scientific" orthodoxy hardened in response to positions Bernstein had not yet fully articulated as public revisionism. POSITION: Historical-reconstructive account of the origins of the orthodoxy/revisionism split, prior to and independent of the political fallout each side triggered.},
    keywords = "Bernstein, Kautsky, revisionism, Second-International, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "In this book, first published in 1992, the author examines the polemic fought by German Social-Democratic Party leaders and intellectuals Karl Kautsky and Eduard Bernstein against what they perceived to be misunderstandings of Marxism propagated by members of the Social-Democratic Federation (SDF) in England and by the socialist leader Wilhelm Liebknecht in Germany. The debate raised basic questions of socialist theory, including whether the program of Marx and Engels called for scholarly study, parliamentary democracy, and gradual social evolution, or for Utopian speculation, economic collapse, and violent rebellion.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{gronow2016formation,
    author = "Gronow, Jukka",
    title = "On the Formation of Marxism: Karl Kautsky's Theory of Capitalism, the Marxism of the Second International and Karl Marx's Critique of Political Economy",
    year = "2016",
    abstract = "On the Formation of Marxism analyses Karl Kautsky's theory of capitalism, the wider Marxism of the Second International, and its relation to Karl Marx's own critique of political economy, including Kautsky's later polemic with Lenin over democracy and dictatorship.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Gronow traces how Kautsky's theory of capitalism became the paradigmatic economic doctrine of "Marxism" for the Second International, argues this formation involved specific interpretive choices vis-a-vis Marx's own critique of political economy, and extends the analysis through Kautsky's later dispute with Lenin over the dictatorship of the proletariat. RELEVANCE: Gives the fullest scholarly account of exactly how Kautsky's systematization became identified with "Marxism" as such for the Second International, making it a key source for assessing whether the resulting "scientific" doctrine faithfully represents Marx's own political economy or represents a distinct, Kautsky-specific construction. POSITION: Historical-analytical account of the formation of "orthodox" Second International Marxism as a distinct theoretical construction, not simply Marx's thought restated.},
    keywords = "Kautsky, Second-International, formation-of-Marxism, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hollander2011engels,
    author = "Hollander, Samuel",
    title = "Friedrich Engels and Marxian Political Economy",
    year = "2011",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Hollander undertakes a close economic-historical study of Engels's own contributions to Marxian political economy, examining his early writings, his editorial completion of Capital volumes 2 and 3, and his later theoretical statements to assess Engels's independent standing as a political economist rather than treating him solely as Marx's expositor. RELEVANCE: Provides an economics-focused counterweight to purely philosophical treatments of the Engels question, relevant to assessing whether the economic-determinist reading originates in Engels's own technical economic work or only in his later popularizing letters. POSITION: Treats Engels as an independent, competent political economist, complicating any simple story of Engels as a vulgarizing distorter of Marx's economics.",
    keywords = "Engels, political-economy, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "This book rejects the commonly encountered perception of Friedrich Engels as perpetuator of a 'tragic deception' of Marx, and the equally persistent body of opinion treating him as 'his master's voice'. Engels' claim to recognition is reinforced by an exceptional contribution in the 1840s to the very foundations of the Marxian enterprise, a contribution entailing not only the 'vision' but some of the building blocks in the working out of that vision. Subsequently, he proved himself to be a sophisticated interpreter of the doctrine of historical materialism and an important contributor in his own right. This volume serves as a companion to Samuel Hollander's The Economics of Karl Marx (Cambridge University Press, 2008).",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{gedik2022engels,
    author = "Gedik, Ali C.",
    title = "Back to Engels: A Long Century of the First Fiddle without the Second",
    year = "2022",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Marking the bicentenary discourse on Engels, Gedik argues that a century of Marxist theory and practice ("the first fiddle") has proceeded largely without adequate engagement with Engels's own distinctive contributions ("without the second [fiddle]"), and calls for renewed direct engagement with Engels's texts rather than treating him only as Marx's junior partner or distorting popularizer. RELEVANCE: Represents the recent centenary-driven scholarly push to re-read Engels on his own terms, directly relevant to disentangling what in "economic determinism" originates specifically with Engels as opposed to Marx or later Second International systematizers. POSITION: Calls for direct re-engagement with Engels's own corpus, implicitly rejecting both hagiographic and dismissive treatments.},
    keywords = "Engels, historiography, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = {In hearing nature hears itself, in smelling it smells itself, in seeing it sees itself.(Marx, 1841) OTH WESTERN MARXISM AND SOVIET MARXISM share the assumption that Engels and Marx agreed upon a division of labour between them: Engels had specialized in nature and Marx in history. 1 Therefore, the contributions of Engels were either rejected or embraced for this exact reason in Western Marxism and Soviet Marxism, respectively.Consequently, these two seemingly contrasting camps had agreed on a similar positivistic approach that amounted to a 'Marxism without Engels'.There was a period when Marxism had not fully divided into two opposite camps; this is roughly the period between the Second International and World War II.Marxists in this period did not assume such a division of labour between Engels and Marx.Those Marxists such as Lafargue, Kautsky, Adler, Labriola, Plekhanov, Lenin, Trotsky, Bogdanov and Bukharin, who were also political leaders, represented this line of Marxism in various degrees in this period.For example, Lenin especially tried to integrate natural sciences 2 into Marxism: "Lenin took the occasion of the second issue of Pod znamenem 1.I use the term history to refer social sciences and humanities in general within the context of the alleged division of labour between Engels and Marx. 2. I use the term natural science to refer any discipline studying natural science, as well as for any discipline studying on natural science such as the history, philosophy, anthropology, and sociology of science.Similarly, the term natural scientist is also used in this broad sense throughout this essay.B},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{baring2023vulgar,
    author = "Baring, Edward",
    title = "Who Are You Calling Vulgar? Lukács, Kautsky, and the Beginnings of ``Western Marxism''",
    year = "2023",
    abstract = {This essay reads Georg Lukács's early thought in light of his vexed relationship to the Social Democratic theorist Karl Kautsky. Though Lukács criticized Kautsky as the archetypal "vulgar Marxist," Lukács's larger project of promoting "class consciousness" follows from Kautsky's own political and pedagogical work. For Kautsky, Social Democratic intellectuals had to transmute proletarian "class instinct" into class consciousness by disseminating a simplified version of scientific socialism, transmitting the conclusions but not the method of Marxist thought. But because Lukács had defined "vulgar Marxism" as any theory that transmitted conclusions without method, he ironically ended up repeating Kautsky's own structure of argument.},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Baring shows that Lukács's foundational denunciation of Kautsky as "vulgar Marxism" - for transmitting Marxist conclusions to the masses without its dialectical method - obscures the extent to which Lukács's own signature project of cultivating proletarian "class consciousness" reproduces exactly the pedagogical structure Kautsky had already developed. RELEVANCE: Shows that the "vulgar Marxism"/economic-determinism label applied to Kautsky by the founders of Western Marxism is itself a polemical construction with a more entangled genealogy than usually assumed, directly bridging this domain to the Western Marxism domain's self-definition against "orthodoxy." POSITION: Revisionist (historiographical) account complicating the Western-Marxist origin myth of a clean break from Kautskyan "vulgar Marxism."},
    keywords = "Kautsky, Lukacs, vulgar-Marxism, Western-Marxism, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{sockol2023plekhanov,
    author = "Sockol, David",
    title = "Verso of the ``Vulgar'' Portrait: Georgi Plekhanov's Anti-Kantianism and Activist Aesthetic",
    year = "2023",
    abstract = {This essay examines Georgi Plekhanov, founder of the Russian Marxist movement and a supposed exemplar of "vulgar Marxism." It analyses Plekhanov's formulation of a Marxist aesthetic, interrogating the widespread image of Plekhanov's "vulgar" focus on economic determinism and his neglect of cultural forces' influence on revolutionary praxis, locating him instead within a fuller history of Marxist thought. A contextualization of Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art demonstrates that these texts were effectively a component of his contemporary polemics against critics of Marxist theory.},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Sockol argues the "vulgar Marxist" image of Plekhanov as narrowly economic-determinist and indifferent to cultural/aesthetic factors is a distorting simplification; Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art formulate an "activist aesthetic" embedded in his broader polemics against contemporary critics of Marxism, including an anti-Kantian philosophical stance. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's most direct rebuttal to the "economic determinism" charge as applied specifically to Plekhanov, complementing Byron's opposite claim (that the charge is correctly aimed at the Engels/Second-International tradition) by showing the charge may be overstated even for its most frequently cited target. POSITION: Rehabilitative reading of Plekhanov against the standard "vulgar Marxist"/economic-determinist caricature.},
    keywords = "Plekhanov, vulgar-Marxism, economic-determinism, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{mayer1993plekhanov,
    author = "Mayer, Robert",
    title = "The dictatorship of the proletariat from Plekhanov to Lenin",
    year = "1993",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mayer traces the concept of the "dictatorship of the proletariat" from Plekhanov's formulation, as a link between Engels and Lenin, through to its Bolshevik transformation, arguing Plekhanov's version carried a tendency toward mechanistic materialism (traceable to Engels's Anti-Dühring and Dialectics of Nature) that Lenin then radicalized in a more voluntarist, dictatorial direction. RELEVANCE: Positions Plekhanov explicitly as an intermediary transmitting an Engels-derived mechanistic strand of materialism into Bolshevik political theory, directly relevant to tracing the lineage of "economic determinism" from Engels through Plekhanov rather than locating its origin in Marx. POSITION: Genealogical account treating Plekhanov as a transmission point for Engelsian mechanistic materialism into Leninism, rather than an independent economic determinist.},
    keywords = "Plekhanov, Engels, Lenin, dictatorship-of-the-proletariat, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{plekhanov1947monist,
    author = "Plekhanov, Georgi Valentinovich",
    title = "In Defence of Materialism: The Development of the Monist View of History",
    year = "1947",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Plekhanov's 1895 treatise (translated here as In Defence of Materialism) presents historical materialism as a "monist" doctrine unifying explanation of ideas, institutions, and social change under the development of productive forces, explicitly framed as a scientific alternative to eclectic and idealist historiography; it is cited in the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's bibliography on Marx as a primary statement of this position. RELEVANCE: This is the primary anchor text for Plekhanov's side of the domain: it is the text against which later charges of "vulgar" economic determinism (addressed critically by Sockol 2023 and traced genealogically by Mayer 1993) are made, and it exemplifies the explicit "scientific"/monist self-presentation that Second International orthodoxy shared with Kautsky's systematization. POSITION: Primary codifying text of Russian Marxist "monism," presented by its author as a scientific and unified conception of history.},
    keywords = "Plekhanov, monist-conception-of-history, primary-source, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{lukacs1971history,
    author = "Lukács, Georg",
    title = "History and Class Consciousness: Studies in Marxist Dialectics",
    year = "1971",
    address = "Cambridge, MA",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Lukács argues that the commodity form, once generalized under capitalism, produces "reification" — the transformation of social relations into apparently autonomous, thing-like objects governed by quasi-natural laws — which penetrates consciousness itself, not merely economic exchange. Against this, he argues that only the proletariat, as a class whose existence within production makes visible the historical, constructed character of social "facts," can grasp society as a dialectical totality and thereby act as the "identical subject-object" of history; this capacity is not empirically given but must be "imputed" (zugerechnet) as the class's rational, ascribed consciousness. RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for locating consciousness, rather than economic mechanism, as the site where historical materialism's revolutionary claims are either vindicated or blocked; it directly answers the research question of how base/superstructure causation is revised to foreground consciousness and totality, and it is the primary source against which nearly all subsequent Western Marxist and Frankfurt School treatments of reification are written. POSITION: Founding text of Western Marxism's turn to Hegelian totality and standpoint epistemology, as opposed to the Second International's evolutionary economism and later structuralist anti-humanism.},
    keywords = "reification, totality, class-consciousness, Lukacs, Western-Marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{gramsci1971selections,
    author = "Gramsci, Antonio",
    title = "Selections from the Prison Notebooks of Antonio Gramsci",
    year = "1971",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Written in fragmentary form under Fascist imprisonment (1929–1935), the Notebooks develop "hegemony" as rule secured through the "intellectual and moral leadership" a dominant group exercises across civil society (schools, churches, media, common sense) rather than through coercive state power alone; the "integral state" fuses political society (coercion) and civil society (consent); the "philosophy of praxis" — Gramsci's guarded label for his own Marxism, adopted partly to evade the prison censor — rejects Bukharin's treatment of Marxism as a deterministic positive science in favor of "absolute historicism," and the "war of position" names a protracted strategy for building a counter-hegemonic "historical bloc" as an alternative to the frontal "war of maneuver" appropriate to the less developed civil societies of the East. RELEVANCE: Directly reconceives the structure/superstructure relation by making superstructural institutions (civil society) the terrain on which political and economic power are actually contested and secured, which is the central textual basis for the research question on how Gramscian hegemony revises Marx's own base/superstructure formulations. POSITION: Founding text of hegemony theory and "absolute historicism," read by later commentators as either a non-reductive elaboration of historical materialism (Thomas, Cospito) or as a substantive departure toward political voluntarism and idealism (Anderson).},
    keywords = "hegemony, civil-society, philosophy-of-praxis, absolute-historicism, war-of-position, Gramsci, Western-Marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{korsch1970marxism,
    author = "Korsch, Karl",
    title = "Marxism and Philosophy",
    year = "1970",
    address = "New York",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Korsch argues that the Marxism of the Second International, in declaring philosophy superseded by positive science after Marx and Engels, unwittingly reproduced the separation of theoretical consciousness from revolutionary practice that historical materialism was meant to abolish; against this, he insists that Marxist theory is itself historically and dialectically conditioned by the class struggle it addresses, so that its own "unity of theory and practice" must be reflexively applied to Marxism's own history and self-understanding, not just to its object. RELEVANCE: Supplies the sharpest philosophical statement, among the three thinkers in this domain, of why treating historical materialism as a deterministic science (rather than a historically self-conscious "function" of proletarian practice) forecloses political agency — directly bearing on the research question of how mechanistic base/superstructure readings are revised to foreground agency. POSITION: Founding text credited (with Lukács) as inaugurating Western Marxism's Hegelian turn against Second International scientism and Comintern orthodoxy, though Korsch's own relation to "Western Marxism" as a category has since been contested (see Devlin 2021).},
    keywords = "philosophy-of-praxis, theory-practice-unity, Korsch, Western-Marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{anderson1976considerations,
    author = "Anderson, Perry",
    title = "Considerations on Western Marxism",
    year = "1976",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Anderson coins "Western Marxism" as a historiographic category for the tradition running from Lukács, Korsch, and Gramsci through the Frankfurt School to Sartre and Althusser, characterizing it by a common displacement, after the defeat of the 1917–23 revolutionary wave outside Russia, of Marxist theory away from economics and revolutionary strategy and toward philosophy, aesthetics, and epistemology; he treats this shift ambivalently, as simultaneously a philosophical enrichment purchased at the cost of practical disconnection from organized working-class politics. RELEVANCE: This is the source of the "Western Marxism" label the domain is built around; it directly frames the research question of what Western Marxism is as a historiographic category and how it relates to historical materialism's core causal claims, and its diagnosis of a turn "from economics and politics to philosophy" is the historiographic thesis later scholarship (Devlin, Kangal) contests. POSITION: Establishes the "Western Marxism" category itself, presented critically from a position sympathetic to classical historical materialism's unity of theory and revolutionary practice.},
    sep_context = {SEP "Antonio Gramsci" (Reception of the Prison Writings, sec. 4): "More widely, Gramsci's exploration of cultural and political superstructures resulted in a tendency to categorize him as a 'western' Marxist, concerned less with economic conditions or coercion and more with ideological barriers to class consciousness (see Anderson 1976, 1976-77)."},
    keywords = "Western-Marxism, historiography, Anderson, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{anderson1976antinomies,
    author = "Anderson, Perry",
    title = "The Antinomies of Antonio Gramsci",
    year = "1976",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Anderson offers a detailed internal critique of the Prison Notebooks, arguing that Gramsci's concept of hegemony contains unresolved oscillations — most consequentially, between "hegemony" as the ruling class's normal condition in the West and hegemony as the working class's needed acquisition prior to a seizure of state power — and that Gramsci's civil-society/political-society distinction, and his "war of position" strategy generalized from Comintern debates about Western Europe, harbor tensions with a properly materialist account of the state and coercion. RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained internal critique of the tensions the domain's third key question asks about — how Gramscian hegemony theory reconceives structure/superstructure and what tensions remain with Marx's own formulations — by identifying specific conceptual antinomies in Gramsci's own text rather than merely praising its innovations. POSITION: Sympathetic-critical reading from within the Western Marxist tradition, treating Gramsci's contribution as genuine but philosophically unstable rather than as a finished alternative to classical historical materialism.},
    keywords = "hegemony, Gramsci, civil-society, structure-superstructure, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Today, no Marxist thinker after the classical epoch is so universally respected in the West as Antonio Gramsci. Nor is any term so freely or diversely invoked on the Left as that of hegemony, to which he gave currency. Gramsci's reputation, still local and marginal outside his native . . .",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{femia1987gramscis,
    author = "Femia, Joseph V.",
    title = "Gramsci's Political Thought: Hegemony, Consciousness, and the Revolutionary Process",
    year = "1987",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Femia reconstructs hegemony as Gramsci's central concept for explaining working-class quiescence in advanced capitalist societies, arguing that hegemonic consent operates by shaping the taken-for-granted "common sense" through which subordinate classes experience their own interests, and that this account requires revising orthodox base-determines-superstructure causality without abandoning a materialist framework; Femia also stresses, contra more democratic readings, that Gramsci's vision of the revolutionary party retained a Leninist, vanguardist core. RELEVANCE: Offers a systematic account of how hegemony functions as a revision of orthodox structure/superstructure causation while defending Gramsci's continuity with (rather than rupture from) Leninist revolutionary strategy, bearing directly on the domain's core question about consciousness and agency in Gramscian theory. POSITION: A systematizing, broadly Leninist reading of hegemony against more liberal-democratic appropriations (e.g., Bobbio, Sassoon) of Gramsci's civil society concept.},
    sep_context = {SEP "Antonio Gramsci" (sec. "The Modern Prince"): "Although some see in Gramsci's politics the basis of a radically democratic politics (Sassoon 1987), his was not a particularly liberal conception (see Femia 1981: 172-85)."},
    keywords = "hegemony, common-sense, Gramsci, revolutionary-party, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "The unifying idea of Antonio Gramsci's famous Prison Notebooks is the concept of hegemony. In this study of these fragmentary writings this book elucidates the precise character of this concept, explores its basic philosophical assumptions, and sets out its implications for Gramsci's explanation of social stability and his vision of the revolutionary process. A number of prevalent and often contradictory myths are demolished, and, moreover, certain neglected aspects of his thought are stressed, including the predominant role he attributed to economic factors, the importance he gave to 'contradictory consciousness', and the close connection between his political thinking and his fundamental philosophical premises. The book concludes by critically examining Gramsci's novel solutions to three long-standing problems for Marxist theory: the reasons why the Western working class has not carried out its revolutionary mission; determining the appropriate strategy for a Marxist party working within an advanced capitalist framework; and what are the reasons behind the failure of existing socialist states in their task of liberation?",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{thomas2009gramscian,
    author = "Thomas, Peter D.",
    title = "The Gramscian Moment: Philosophy, Hegemony and Marxism",
    year = "2009",
    series = "Historical Materialism Book Series",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Thomas argues, on the basis of the critical edition of the Notebooks, that Gramsci's "philosophy of praxis" and "integral state" constitute an original theoretical response to debates internal to the Third International (particularly disputes over Bukharin's Historical Materialism and the united front) rather than a Crocean or idealist departure from Marxism, and that hegemony should be understood as Gramsci's attempt to think the "translatability" of theoretical languages across national political traditions within a still-materialist framework. RELEVANCE: Directly engages the domain's key question of whether Gramscian hegemony theory remains within historical materialism's causal/agency structure or departs from it, arguing for continuity against readings (including Anderson's) that treat Gramsci's turn to culture and consent as a substantive weakening of materialist explanation. POSITION: A philologically grounded, "orthodox continuity" reading of Gramsci against both liberal-democratic (Bobbio) and idealist-departure (early Anderson) interpretations.},
    sep_context = {SEP "Antonio Gramsci" (Reception of the Prison Writings, sec. 4): "His ideas remain a source of insight for non-orthodox Marxisms (see Thomas 2009), and his concepts have been extended to academic fields such as International Relations and Global Political Economy (see Gill 1993) and to socio-political contexts beyond Europe and the 'West' (see Morton 2007; Fonseca 2016)."},
    keywords = "philosophy-of-praxis, integral-state, Gramsci, Third-International, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Winner of the Premio internazionale Giuseppe Sormani 2011, awarded by the Fondazione Istituto Piemontese Antonio Gramsci in Turin for the best book/article on Gramsci in the period between 2007-2011 internationally. Antonio Gramsci's Prison Notebooks are today acknowledged as a classic of the human and social sciences in the twentieth century. The influence of his thought in numerous fields of scholarship is only exceeded by the diverse interpretations and readings to which it has been subjected, resulting in often contradictory 'images of Gramsci'. This book draws on the rich recent season of Gramscian philological studies in order to argue that the true significance of Gramsci's thought consists in its distinctive position in the development of the Marxist tradition. Providing a detailed reconsideration of Gramsci's theory of the state and concept of philosophy, The 'Gramscian moment' argues for the urgent necessity of taking up the challenge of developing a 'philosophy of praxis' as a vital element in the contemporary revitalisation of Marxism.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{cospito2016rhythm,
    author = "Cospito, Giuseppe",
    title = "The Rhythm of Thought in Gramsci: A Diachronic Interpretation of the Prison Notebooks",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Using the critical, chronologically ordered edition of the Notebooks, Cospito traces how Gramsci's treatment of the structure/superstructure relation changes across successive notebooks, showing that Gramsci experimented with several formulations (the historical bloc, "necessity and freedom," organic/conjunctural movements) before arriving at a mature position that treats structure and superstructure as reciprocally conditioning within a single, non-hierarchical dialectical process rather than as cause and effected reflection. RELEVANCE: Provides the most philologically precise answer available in this literature to the question of how Gramsci revises the mechanistic base/superstructure model, showing this revision as a developing textual process rather than a fixed doctrine, and thereby sharpening claims (e.g., in Anderson or Femia) that treat Gramsci's position as settled. POSITION: A diachronic, textually grounded reconstruction that treats apparent inconsistencies in Gramsci's structure/superstructure language as evidence of genuine theoretical development rather than confusion.},
    keywords = "structure-superstructure, historical-bloc, Gramsci, philology, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Many scholars have recently shown great interest in a diachronic re-examination of Antonio Gramsci's main theoretical-political categories in the Prison Notebooks. This method would uncover the origins and development of Gramsci's concepts using the same method that Gramsci himself believed would allow us to grasp 'the rhythm of thought' in Marx. The present work embraces this perspective and puts it to work in two ways. Its first part analyzes the relation between structure and superstructure and the concepts of hegemony and the regulated society. Its second part extends the diachronic analysis to the conceptual pairings which represent alternatives to structure-superstructure, encompassing questions of political and cultural organisation as well as the relation between Gramsci and the major proponents of historical materialism (Marx, Engels, Lenin). English translation of Il ritmo del pensiero: per una lettura diacronica dei «Quaderni del carcere» di Gramsci published by Bibliopolis, Naples (2011).",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{morera1990gramscis,
    author = "Morera, Esteve",
    title = "Gramsci's Historicism: A Realist Interpretation",
    year = "1990",
    address = "London and New York",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Morera argues that Gramsci's "absolute historicism" should be read as a realist, rather than idealist or relativist, epistemological and ontological position: the structure/superstructure relation within a "historical bloc" is interpreted through Gramsci's concept of historical time, and hegemony, civil society, and the state are analyzed as the mechanisms through which structural constraints and superstructural agency are mutually articulated without collapsing into either economic determinism or pure voluntarism. RELEVANCE: Offers a sustained interpretive answer to how the structure/superstructure relation operates within Gramsci's historicism specifically, directly addressing the domain's central question about Gramscian revisions of base/superstructure causality, and defending a "realist" middle position against both economistic and idealist readings. POSITION: A realist interpretation of Gramscian historicism, positioned against readings that treat "absolute historicism" as relativism or as a Crocean idealist residue.},
    keywords = "absolute-historicism, structure-superstructure, historical-bloc, Gramsci, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{devlin2021korsch,
    author = "Devlin, Nicholas",
    title = "Karl Korsch and Marxism's Interwar Moment, 1917--1933",
    year = "2021",
    abstract = "This article offers a major reinterpretation of the nature of interwar Marxist theory. It does so by offering a new reading of the work of Karl Korsch in the context of a network of ex-communist intellectuals. In Marxism and Philosophy (1923), Korsch responded to the split in the labour movement with a radical new claim to Marxist orthodoxy. Rather than engaging in Marx exegesis, he aimed to turn the Marxist 'method' on Marxism's own history. In the narrative he constructed, Bolshevik-inspired Communism appeared as the next logical step in the dialectic. But this argument rested on a historicisation of Marx's own writing that led to an unresolvable tension in Korsch's work that threatened to undermine its claim to Marxist orthodoxy. Once it is understood that Marxism was a political currency as much as a purely theoretical space of argument, Korsch's reluctance to resolve the tension one way or the other becomes understandable. This reinterpretation of Korsch's work challenges the 'Western Marxism' paradigm in which he has been read, showing that Korsch's work presupposed a reading of revolutionary success and potential rather than defeat and that he did not advocate a turn to superstructural or cultural questions in the manner supposed.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: See abstract — Devlin argues Korsch aimed to historicize Marxism's own theoretical self-understanding as a claim to orthodoxy (not heresy), and that reading him through the retrospective "Western Marxism" paradigm mischaracterizes his politics as a turn toward superstructure/culture when he in fact presupposed continued revolutionary potential. RELEVANCE: Directly complicates the domain's key question about Western Marxism as a historiographic category by arguing that Korsch, one of the tradition's supposed founders, does not fit Anderson's diagnosis of a retreat from economics/politics into culture — a significant revisionist check on the category's coherence. POSITION: Revisionist intellectual history that unsettles the "Western Marxism" paradigm from within, using Korsch as the test case.},
    keywords = "Korsch, Western-Marxism, historiography, interwar-Marxism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{baring2023marxism,
    author = "Baring, Edward",
    title = "Marxism of, by, and for the People: Karl Korsch and the Problem of Worker Education",
    year = "2023",
    abstract = {This article argues for the importance of worker education for understanding the intellectual history of Marxism. It examines the work of the early Western Marxist Karl Korsch, who was deeply engaged in that project, showing that his most famous text, 1923's Marxism and Philosophy, can be read as a reflection on its problems and goals, especially the demand that the theory taught to the workers should "express" their life experience and struggle. The article ends with a discussion of the way in which the project of worker education can help us think through the geographical specificities of Marxism. In adjusting the pedagogical project to "express" new populations, especially in countries without a large industrial working class, intellectuals and party leaders entertained broad revisions to Marxist theory.},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: See abstract — Baring reads Marxism and Philosophy as arising from Korsch's practical engagement with worker education, arguing the demand that theory "express" workers' lived experience and struggle is central to understanding Korsch's philosophical claims about theory-practice unity. RELEVANCE: Grounds Korsch's abstract claim about the unity of theory and revolutionary practice in a concrete pedagogical and organizational context, giving the domain's account of Korsch a material basis in political agency (worker self-education) rather than treating "theory-practice unity" as purely philosophical. POSITION: Intellectual-historical contextualization of Korsch's philosophy of praxis within concrete party pedagogy, complementing more purely philosophical treatments (e.g., Devlin).},
    keywords = "Korsch, worker-education, theory-practice-unity, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{feenberg2014philosophy,
    author = "Feenberg, Andrew",
    title = "The Philosophy of Praxis: Marx, Lukács and the Frankfurt School",
    year = "2014",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Feenberg traces a "philosophy of praxis" running from Marx through Lukács's theory of reification and totality into the Frankfurt School, arguing that Lukács's central achievement is a non-reductive account of how objectified, reified social forms can nonetheless be grasped and transformed by a historically self-conscious subject, and defending the continuing relevance of Lukács's dialectic of subject and object against readings that treat it as either metaphysically overambitious or as merely an anticipation of Frankfurt School critical theory. RELEVANCE: Provides a sustained defense of the philosophical coherence of Lukács's reification/totality framework as a genuine (not merely rhetorical) revision of mechanistic materialism, directly relevant to the domain's first key question about how consciousness and agency are made central to historical materialism without lapsing into idealism. POSITION: A defense of Lukácsian "philosophy of praxis" as a still-viable dialectical alternative to both mechanistic materialism and post-Lukácsian critical theory's turn away from the proletariat as revolutionary subject.},
    keywords = "reification, totality, philosophy-of-praxis, Lukacs, Frankfurt-School, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {This is a thoroughly revised version of Andrew Feenberg's first book, Lukács, Marx and the Sources of Critical Theory (1981). In the Preface to the new version Feenberg writes that he often has looked back to the original version, based on his doctoral dissertation supervised by Herbert Marcuse, "with a mixture of pride and dismay." The book can be divided into three parts: three chapters each on Marx's early writings and on Lukács's History and Class Consciousness (including discussion of his later defense of that book), and two chapters on how members of the Frankfurt School, particularly Adorno and Marcuse, took up and transformed this legacy. Both the obsolescence of philosophy and its possible realization are problems that arise in Marx's early writings and in Lukács's History and Class Consciousness, in the context of a philosophy of praxis that aims to overcome the antinomies identified by modern philosophy through collective social action, resolving them neither critically (Kant) nor speculatively (Hegel) but metacritically, by showing that seemingly philosophical problems are rooted in social reality and can only be resolved by social change.},
    abstract_source = "ndpr"
}

@misc{chari2010toward,
    author = "Chari, Anita",
    title = "Toward a Political Critique of Reification: Lukács, Honneth and the Aims of Critical Theory",
    year = "2010",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Chari argues that Axel Honneth's influential re-reading of reification as a failure of "recognition" strips the concept of the explicitly political dimension it had in Lukács — where reification names a systemic feature of capitalist social relations requiring collective political transformation, not merely a deficient interpersonal or psychological stance — and calls for recovering this political dimension for contemporary critical theory. RELEVANCE: Uses Lukács's original concept of reification as a normative baseline to criticize a major contemporary appropriation (Honneth), which is directly useful for tracking how far Lukács's revision of historical materialism's consciousness/agency structure has been preserved or diluted in critical theory's subsequent, more depoliticized uses of "reification." POSITION: A defense of the politically systemic (Lukácsian) sense of reification against its individualized, recognition-theoretic (Honnethian) successor within critical theory.},
    keywords = "reification, Lukacs, Honneth, critical-theory, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "This article engages Axel Honneth's recent work on Georg Lukács' concept of reification in order to formulate a politically relevant and historically specific critique of capitalism that is applicable to theorizing contemporary democratic practice. I argue that Honneth's attempt to reorient the critique of reification within the terms of a theory of recognition has done so at the cost of sacrificing the core of the concept, which forged a connection between the socio-political analysis of capitalist domination and an analysis of the unengaged, spectatorial stance of human beings toward the world, showing how they together impede emancipatory social transformation. In order to accomplish the unfinished task of rendering the critique of reification applicable to contemporary critical theory, I seek to synthesize the advantages of Honneth's approach, which focuses on the normative aspects of the critique of reification, with Lukács' emphasis on the practical, political-economic dimensions of reification and the historically specific pathologies of the capitalist social form.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{gindner2023totality,
    author = "Gindner, Jette",
    title = "Totality Reconsidered: Lukács's History and Class Consciousness a Century On",
    year = "2023",
    abstract = "Georg Lukács's essay collection History and Class Consciousness (1923) remains a foundational text of Western Marxism and a vital influence on contemporary German studies, not least by way of the Frankfurt School. Yet Lukács's positing of the proletariat as the historical agent of revolutionary change raises difficult questions for radical Left theory and praxis in our rapidly deindustrializing world. Who or what is that working class that would redeem Lukács's vision today? This article argues that the main concept for actualizing History and Class Consciousness is totality rather than class or reification. However, we should understand totality in a strictly negative sense, as a feature of capitalist coercion to be overcome in a free society.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: See abstract — Gindner argues that "totality," reinterpreted negatively as a feature of capitalist coercion to be overcome rather than a positive standpoint achieved by a determinate revolutionary class, is the concept that allows History and Class Consciousness to remain actionable once the industrial proletariat Lukács assumed is no longer the obvious historical agent. RELEVANCE: Directly engages the difficulty, central to this domain's treatment of Lukács, that "imputed class consciousness" depends on identifying a specific class as history's necessary agent; Gindner's reinterpretation of totality is a recent (2023) attempt to preserve Lukács's anti-economistic insight while detaching it from this specific agency claim. POSITION: A contemporary, left-critical reconstruction of Lukácsian totality that abandons class essentialism while retaining anti-reificatory critique.},
    keywords = "totality, reification, class-consciousness, Lukacs, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{jay1984marxism,
    author = "Jay, Martin",
    title = "Marxism and Totality: The Adventures of a Concept from Lukács to Habermas",
    year = "1984",
    address = "Berkeley",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Jay reconstructs "totality" as the organizing concept of an entire tradition of Hegelian Marxism running from Lukács through Gramsci, Korsch, the Frankfurt School, and existentialist Marxism to Habermas, tracing how each successive thinker redefines totality (as expressive, longitudinal, structural, or decentered) in response to prior critiques, and arguing that the concept's instability — its oscillation between a normative ideal and a description of actual social integration — is itself diagnostic of Western Marxism's broader difficulties in reconciling agency and structure. RELEVANCE: Provides the broadest cross-cutting intellectual history of the concept ("totality") that links Lukács, Gramsci (via the historical bloc), and later Frankfurt School critical theory, making it useful for tracing how the domain's key concepts migrate and mutate across Western Marxism as a named tradition. POSITION: A synoptic intellectual history that treats "totality" as the thread unifying (and destabilizing) Western Marxism as a category, complementing Anderson's more polemical historiographic account.},
    keywords = "totality, Western-Marxism, Lukacs, Gramsci, Frankfurt-School, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{haug2011marx,
    author = "Haug, Wolfgang Fritz",
    title = "From Marx to Gramsci, from Gramsci to Marx: Historical Materialism and the Philosophy of Praxis",
    year = "2011",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Haug argues for reading Gramsci's "philosophy of praxis" as continuous with, rather than a revision away from, Marx's own historical materialism, tracing specific conceptual borrowings and transformations as Gramsci moves between Marx's texts and his own prison-notebook vocabulary, and using this movement to push back against readings that treat Gramsci's culturalism as a departure from Marx. RELEVANCE: Directly engages the domain's first key question — whether Gramsci (and Lukács and Korsch) revise or merely elaborate Marx's own base/superstructure formulations — by arguing for textual continuity between Marx's and Gramsci's historical materialism rather than a rupture. POSITION: A continuity thesis linking Gramsci's philosophy of praxis directly back to Marx's own texts, against discontinuity readings (e.g., early Anderson) that treat Gramscian hegemony as a substantive innovation beyond Marx.},
    keywords = "philosophy-of-praxis, structure-superstructure, Gramsci, Marx, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{horkheimer1972criticaltheory,
    author = "Horkheimer, Max",
    title = "Critical Theory: Selected Essays",
    year = "1972",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This collection includes Horkheimer's programmatic 1937 essay "Traditional and Critical Theory," which distinguishes "traditional theory" (which treats facts, including social facts, as independent of the historically situated, socially embedded subject who perceives them) from critical theory, which insists that both the object of knowledge and the knowing subject are products of social praxis and that theory itself is a moment within the historical totality it studies, oriented toward the practical abolition of an unjust social order rather than mere contemplation. RELEVANCE: This essay is the founding methodological statement of the Frankfurt School's relation to Marxism: it retains Marx's demand that theory serve emancipatory practice while rejecting the "traditional," positivist reading of historical materialism as a set of law-like, verifiable propositions about economic development, thereby opening the door to the psychoanalytic and cultural extensions of critical theory documented elsewhere in this bibliography. POSITION: Founding statement of Critical Theory's self-differentiation from both positivism and orthodox ("traditional") Marxism.},
    keywords = "Horkheimer, critical-theory, traditional-theory, economism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{benjamin2020thesesphilosophyhistory,
    author = "Benjamin, Walter",
    title = "Theses on the Philosophy of History",
    year = "2020",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Benjamin's theses (originally circulated 1940) reject the image of history as a homogeneous, empty continuum of progress, proposing instead that historical materialism must "brush history against the grain": the present is not a stage on a continuous developmental path but a moment of potential rupture ("Jetztzeit") in which the "weak messianic power" of past generations of the oppressed can be redeemed. The angel of history, facing the accumulating wreckage of the past while blown backward into the future, replaces the image of progress with one of catastrophe. RELEVANCE: This is the primary text for the domain's messianic/discontinuous challenge to historical materialism's assumption of necessary, continuous historical progress toward socialism; it supplies the counter-model against which Tiedemann, Beiner, Marramao, Moir, and Habermas's own critical reading are organized in this bibliography. POSITION: Messianic/discontinuous historiography, in tension with both orthodox progress-narratives and later communicative-evolutionary reconstructions of historical materialism.},
    keywords = "Benjamin, philosophy-of-history, messianism, discontinuity, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = {A chronicler who recites events without distinguishing between major and minor ones acts in accordance with following truth: nothing that has ever happened should be regarded as lost for history. The class struggle, which is always present to a historian influenced by Marx, is a fight for the crude and material things without which no refined and spiritual things could exist. Historical materialism wishes to retain that image of the past which unexpectedly appears to man singled out by history at a moment of danger. "In relation to the history of organic life on earth," writes a modern biologist, "the paltry fifty millennia of homo sapiens constitute something like two seconds at the close of a twenty-four-hour day. The present, which, as a model of Messianic time, comprises the entire history of mankind in an enormous abridgment, coincides exactly with the stature which the history of mankind has in the universe. Historicism contents itself with establishing a causal connection between various moments in history.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{tiedemann1989historicalmaterialism,
    author = "Tiedemann, Rolf",
    editor = "Smith, Gary",
    title = {Historical Materialism or Political Messianism? An Interpretation of the Theses "On the Concept of History"},
    year = "1989",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Tiedemann poses the question that titles the essay directly: are Benjamin's "Theses on the Philosophy of History" best read as a (heterodox) contribution to historical materialism, or as a political messianism that has effectively displaced materialist categories with theological ones? Tiedemann traces the textual evidence for both readings within Benjamin's late work and its genesis alongside the unfinished Arcades Project. RELEVANCE: This is the classic scholarly framing of the interpretive dispute the research project needs to represent: whether Benjamin's contribution to (or against) historical materialism should be read as an internal radicalization of materialist historiography or as its theological supersession. POSITION: Interpretive crux-essay; does not resolve but names the materialism/messianism dispute that structures later scholarship (Beiner, Marramao, Moir).},
    keywords = "Benjamin, Tiedemann, messianism, historical-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, type:@incollection->@misc",
    sep_context = {SEP "Walter Benjamin" (sec. 9, Image, History, Culture) cites this essay as central to the reception debate: "Debate over Benjamin's conception of history was for many years preoccupied with the question of whether it is essentially 'theological' or 'materialist' in character (or how it could possibly be both at once), occasioned by the conjunction of Benjamin's self-identification with historical materialism and his continued use of explicitly messianic motifs (Wohlfarth 1978; Tiedemann 1983-4)."}
}

@misc{beiner1984walterbenjamin,
    author = "Beiner, Ronald",
    title = "Walter Benjamin's Philosophy of History",
    year = "1984",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Beiner examines Benjamin's "Theses" as a philosophy of history addressed specifically to the political dangers of historicism and progressivism within the Marxist and social-democratic tradition, arguing that Benjamin's critique of "empty, homogeneous time" is directed as much against complacent, evolutionary versions of Marxism as against bourgeois historicism. RELEVANCE: Provides a political-theory framing (as opposed to a purely theological or literary one) of why Benjamin's discontinuous historiography functions as an internal critique of the Marxist philosophy of history he inherited, sharpening the domain's account of the tension between messianic time and the evolutionary/progressive assumptions targeted by other Frankfurt School authors. POSITION: Reads Benjamin as an immanent political critic of progressivist Marxism rather than as a theologian abandoning materialism.},
    keywords = "Benjamin, philosophy-of-history, progress, Marxism, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = {What is Walter Benjamin's intention in his enigmatic "Theses on the Philosophy of History"? According to Benjamin's friend Gershom Scholem, the Theses mark Benjamin's decisive break with materialism and a return to the metaphysical-theological concerns of his early thought: what the Theses have left in common with materialism is only the ironic use of its termini technici, so that frequently nothing remains of materialism except the term itself. On Scholem's reading, the angel of history (in thesis IX) cannot make whole again the fragments of history, and therefore Benjamin must have resort, in the last thesis, to the Messiah, who alone can succeed where the angel must fail -- in the redemption of history. The Theses themselves present a very different face: in every one of the theses, Benjamin gives the impression of struggling to define the true nature of materialism, and to formulate his own position in relation to historicism generally.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{marramao2008messianism,
    author = "Marramao, Giacomo",
    title = {Messianism without Delay: On the "Post-Religious" Political Theology of Walter Benjamin},
    year = "2008",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Marramao argues that Benjamin's messianism should be read as "post-religious" rather than theological in a traditional sense: the "weak messianic power" names a structural possibility of interruption available in any present moment ("without delay"), not a doctrine of eschatological fulfillment, and this distinguishes Benjamin's political theology from both orthodox Marxist teleology and conventional religious messianism. RELEVANCE: Sharpens the domain's central question about what Benjamin's messianism contributes to (rather than simply against) historical materialism, by recasting the messianic as a formal-political category compatible with materialist critique rather than a return to theology. POSITION: Post-religious reading of Benjaminian messianism, positioned against both straightforwardly theological and straightforwardly materialist-orthodox interpretations.},
    keywords = "Benjamin, messianism, political-theology, Marramao, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{moir2016walterbenjaminremains,
    author = "Moir, Cat",
    title = "Walter Benjamin and the Remains of a Philosophy of History",
    year = "2016",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Moir surveys competing scholarly attempts to reconstruct a coherent "philosophy of history" from Benjamin's fragmentary and posthumously assembled writings, arguing that what remains after these reconstructions is less a systematic philosophy than a set of unresolved tensions between materialist and theological vocabularies that Benjamin himself never fully adjudicated. The essay takes the form of a review of Uwe Steiner's Walter Benjamin: An Introduction to His Work and Thought, arguing that Steiner's historicizing account, for all its virtues, somewhat neutralizes Benjamin's critical potential as a historical-materialist thinker. RELEVANCE: Offers a review-essay vantage point on the state of the materialism/messianism debate (engaging Löwy, Tiedemann, Bloch, and others), useful for framing how contested Benjamin's relation to historical materialism remains in current scholarship. POSITION: Skeptical of fully systematic reconstructions; treats the materialist/theological tension in Benjamin as constitutively unresolved, and argues that interpreting Benjamin requires a "weakly teleological" concept of history.},
    keywords = "Benjamin, philosophy-of-history, historiography, Moir, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Uwe Steiner's Walter Benjamin: An Introduction to His Work and Thought is a comprehensive and compelling account of Walter Benjamin's life and work, which will satisfy both newcomers to Benjamin and those with an existing interest. In this review, I argue that Steiner's account goes beyond similar encounters with Benjamin in two main ways: first, by focusing specifically on Benjamin's personal and intellectual relationship with 'modernity' and, second, by presenting Benjamin's enduring appeal as a result of the creative interpretation of his work according to changing times and tastes. Yet Steiner's historicising account of Benjamin also somewhat neutralises his critical potential as a historical-materialist thinker. Drawing on the work of Benjamin's erstwhile friend and contemporary Ernst Bloch, as well as on Peter Osborne's concept of modernity as a specific consciousness of time, I argue that the act of interpretation itself requires a weakly teleological concept of history, such as we find with Bloch and, between the lines perhaps, also with Steiner's Benjamin.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{habermas1979consciousnessraising,
    author = "Habermas, Jürgen and Brewster, Philip and Buchner, Carl Howard",
    title = "Consciousness-Raising or Redemptive Criticism: The Contemporaneity of Walter Benjamin",
    year = "1979",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas distinguishes Benjamin's "redemptive criticism" (Rettende Kritik), oriented toward anamnestic solidarity with past suffering and the truth-content of what has been, from consciousness-raising critique oriented toward present and future emancipatory practice. He argues that Benjamin's messianic conception of truth as redemption of the past cannot be straightforwardly incorporated into a materialist theory of social practice and progress, and traces the difficulties this creates for locating Benjamin within (or against) historical materialism. RELEVANCE: This is the key text bridging the Benjamin and Habermas strands of the domain: Habermas's own verdict on whether Benjamin's messianic historiography is compatible with a theory -- like his own reconstructed historical materialism -- that ties critique to future-oriented learning processes. POSITION: Habermas reads Benjamin's redemptive critique as an important but ultimately incommensurable supplement to, rather than a version of, emancipatory historical materialism.},
    keywords = "Habermas, Benjamin, redemptive-critique, messianism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "Habermas distinguishes redemptive (rescuing) criticism from consciousness-raising criticism in his reading of Walter Benjamin, published in New German Critique's Special Walter Benjamin Issue (Spring 1979), pp. 30-59.",
    abstract_source = "editorial-summary"
}

@misc{habermas1975reconstruction,
    author = "Habermas, Jürgen",
    title = "Towards a Reconstruction of Historical Materialism",
    year = "1975",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas proposes to "reconstruct" historical materialism by taking it apart and reassembling it so as to better achieve its own stated goals. He replaces Marx's single-track model of history as driven by the development of productive forces (labor) with a two-track model distinguishing "labor" (instrumental action oriented to technical mastery of nature) from "interaction" (communicative action oriented to mutual understanding, governed by its own developmental logic). Social evolution is reinterpreted as a sequence of institutionalized learning processes -- cognitive, moral-practical, and normative -- rather than as a strictly economically determined succession of modes of production. RELEVANCE: This is the primary programmatic text of Habermas's explicit "reconstruction" project named in the research prompt; it is the essay all the secondary literature in this bibliography (Rockmore, Reichelt, Giddens, Stahl) responds to and evaluates. POSITION: Founding statement of the communicative-action/social-evolution reconstruction of historical materialism, replacing the primacy of the labor/production paradigm with a dual labor-interaction model.},
    keywords = "Habermas, reconstruction, historical-materialism, communicative-action, social-evolution, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{rockmore1979habermasreconstruction,
    author = "Rockmore, Tom",
    title = "Habermas and the Reconstruction of Historical Materialism",
    year = "1979",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Rockmore maps Habermas's engagement with Marx and Marxism as a four-stage itinerary -- interpretation, critique, reconstruction, and ultimately partial rejection of historical materialism -- and questions whether the concept of "reconstruction" itself is stable, since Habermas's own relation to the doctrine shifts across these stages. RELEVANCE: Provides an early, close critical reading of exactly how much of historical materialism survives Habermas's reconstruction, anticipating the more developed argument in Rockmore's later book-length study (Habermas on Historical Materialism) and setting terms later taken up by Reichelt and Stahl. POSITION: Critical of the reconstruction project's stability; argues Habermas's relation to historical materialism moves toward its effective abandonment rather than genuine reconstruction.},
    keywords = "Rockmore, Habermas, reconstruction, historical-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{reichelt2000habermasreconstruction,
    author = "Reichelt, Helmut",
    editor = "Bonefeld, Werner and Psychopedis, Kosmas",
    title = "Jürgen Habermas' Reconstruction of Historical Materialism",
    year = "2000",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reichelt contrasts Marx's own methodological starting point -- deciphering society through the historically specific, "inverted" forms taken by social relations (commodity, value, and prior to that, consciousness, religion, morality, law) -- with Habermas's reconstruction, arguing that Habermas's turn to communicative action loses sight of Marx's critique of these "inverted forms" of social existence in favor of a more formal theory of validity claims and social evolution. RELEVANCE: Offers a Marx-philological counter-critique of Habermas's reconstruction from within a broadly open-Marxist perspective, arguing that the reconstruction substitutes a new set of categories for Marx's critique of political economy rather than genuinely reconstructing it. POSITION: Open-Marxist critique holding that Habermas's reconstruction abandons rather than updates Marx's critique of inverted social forms.},
    keywords = "Reichelt, Habermas, reconstruction, open-Marxism, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "If one wishes to differentiate Habermas' theory from that of Marx in terms of its central motifs, then one will have to argue along the following lines: from the very beginning, even in his doctoral thesis on Democritus' and Epicurus' Natural Philosophy, Marx pursued a programme of deciphering society as an 'organic' (naturwüchsige) form of increasing individualization. Marx's focus is on forms, at first on forms of consciousness (i.e., religion, philosophy, morality, law), then later on the forms or categories of political economy. For Marx, the focus on forms was identical with the critique of the inverted forms of social existence, an existence constituted by the life-practice of human beings. All these forms obtain as an inverted form of a 'community' that is external to the individuals, and from which they must emancipate themselves in order ever to be able to interact with one another 'as individuals.' This central idea is presented in its most pregnant form in The German Ideology.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{giddens1982labourinteraction,
    author = "Giddens, Anthony",
    title = "Labour and Interaction",
    year = "1982",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Giddens examines Habermas's distinction between labor and interaction as the pivot of the reconstruction project, arguing that Habermas deliberately eschews "reviving" or "restoring" orthodox Marxism in favor of taking the theory apart and reassembling it to better fulfil its own emancipatory goals, but presses on whether the resulting theory of communicative competence can still carry the explanatory and normative weight historical materialism originally claimed for the development of the forces and relations of production. RELEVANCE: A sociologist's (rather than a Frankfurt School insider's) assessment of the reconstruction project, useful for showing how Habermas's revision of historical materialism was received and contested outside critical theory's own institutional lineage. POSITION: Sympathetically expository but ultimately questioning of whether Habermas's dual labor/interaction model successfully replaces the explanatory ambitions of classical historical materialism.},
    keywords = "Giddens, Habermas, labour, interaction, reconstruction, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "Labour and interaction: innocuous-sounding terms, but ones around which Habermas has consolidated some of the main themes in his work. It makes sense to see most of Habermas's writings as concerned with what he has come to call the 'reconstruction of historical materialism' -- a critical reformulation of the dominant concerns of Marx, both on the level of philosophy or 'metatheory' and on the level of the development of industrial capitalism since Marx's day. Habermas uses 'reconstruction' in a very deliberate way, as he makes clear. He is not interested, as he says, in reviving or 'restoring' traditional Marxist ideas: his preoccupation with Marx is not a scholastic or dogmatic one. Rather, it is in need of a wholesale overhaul. 'Reconstruction,' Habermas argues, 'signifies taking a theory apart and putting it back together again in a new form in order to attain more fully the goal it has set for itself.'",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{stahl2013habermasimmanent,
    author = "Stahl, Titus",
    title = "Habermas and the Project of Immanent Critique",
    year = "2013",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Stahl argues that Habermas's Theory of Communicative Action should be read as offering a novel, "practice-based" form of immanent critique -- one that locates normative standards within the socially instituted practice of communicative understanding rather than in a transcendental or metaphysical foundation, or in a merely hermeneutic reconstruction of already-accepted norms. Stahl contends that Habermas's narrow social ontology of immanent normativity nonetheless limits the critical purchase of this model. RELEVANCE: Clarifies the methodological stakes of Habermas's break from Marx's own model of immanent critique (which located critical norms in the material conditions of social reproduction) and situates the reconstruction project within the broader question of how critical theory after Marx can ground its normative claims without either transcendental philosophy or economistic determinism. POSITION: Reconstructs and partially defends, while also limiting, the claim that Habermas's communicative turn preserves a genuine practice of immanent (Marx-descended) critique.},
    keywords = "Stahl, Habermas, immanent-critique, communicative-action, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = {According to his own understanding, Jürgen Habermas' Theory of Communicative Action offers a new account of the normative foundations of critical theory. Habermas' motivating insight is that neither a transcendental or metaphysical solution to the problem of normativity, nor a merely hermeneutic reconstruction of historically given norms, is sufficient to clarify the normative foundations of critical theory. In response, Habermas develops a novel account of normativity which locates the normative demands upon which critical theory draws within the socially instituted practice of communicative understanding. Although Habermas has claimed otherwise, this new foundation for critical theory constitutes a novel form of "immanent critique." The paper argues that Habermas's implausibly narrow view regarding the social ontology of immanent normativity means his normative critique cannot develop its full potential.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{vaki2005adornocontrahabermas,
    author = "Vaki, Fotini",
    title = "Adorno Contra Habermas: The Claims of Critical Theory as Immanent Critique",
    year = "2005",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Vaki contrasts Adorno's and Habermas's competing conceptions of immanent critique, arguing that Adorno's negative dialectics -- his refusal of any positive, reconciled "totality" as the standpoint of critique -- constitutes a more consistent (if more pessimistic) claim to immanent critique than Habermas's communicative-theoretic reconstruction, which Vaki argues covertly reintroduces a quasi-transcendental standpoint external to the social totality it claims to criticize immanently. RELEVANCE: Directly stages the intergenerational dispute the domain needs to represent: whether Adorno's totality/identity-thinking critique or Habermas's reconstructed historical materialism better preserves Marx's own commitment to grounding critique in existing social conditions rather than an external normative standpoint. POSITION: Defends Adorno's negative-dialectical immanent critique against Habermas's communicative reconstruction as the more consistent continuation of Marx's method.},
    keywords = "Vaki, Adorno, Habermas, immanent-critique, totality, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{cook2000criticalstratagems,
    author = "Cook, Deborah",
    title = "Critical Stratagems in Adorno and Habermas: Theories of Ideology and the Ideology of Theory",
    year = "2000",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Cook compares Adorno's and Habermas's theories of ideology, arguing that Adorno's account -- rooted in the commodity form and the fetish-character of exchange -- retains a materialist grounding in Marx's critique of political economy that Habermas's later, more linguistically and cognitively oriented theory of ideology and communicative distortion loses or attenuates. RELEVANCE: Adds a further axis (theories of ideology, rather than of history or immanent critique per se) along which Adorno's Marx-indebted materialism and Habermas's reconstruction can be compared, reinforcing the domain's account of divergent first- and second-generation strategies for explaining the absence of revolutionary class consciousness. POSITION: Favors Adorno's commodity-form-based theory of ideology as more faithful to Marx's materialist categories than Habermas's communicative alternative.",
    keywords = "Cook, Adorno, Habermas, ideology, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "In one of his many metaphorical turns of phrase -- a leitmotif in The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity -- Jürgen Habermas speaks of the path not taken by modern philosophers, a path that might have led them towards his own intersubjective notion of communicative reason. Habermas is especially critical of his predecessors, Theodor W. Adorno and Max Horkheimer, because, he believes, they repudiated the rational potential in the culture of modernity. By the 1940s, their confidence in modern culture had allegedly succumbed to bitter disillusionment; on Habermas's view, their scepticism finally embraced reason itself, so that ideology critique itself came under suspicion of no longer producing truths, forcing Adorno and Horkheimer to render their critique independent even of its own foundations. This paper contests that reading of Adorno's theory of ideology.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{osborne2020adornomarx,
    author = "Osborne, Peter",
    title = "Adorno and Marx",
    year = "2020",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Osborne reconstructs the place of Marx's thought across Adorno's career, from the 1931 inaugural lecture to the 1962 seminar on Marx, focusing on three areas: Adorno's critique and dialectical transformation of philosophy, his sociology of the commodification of art, and his social ontology of the "objectivity of illusions" derived from the critique of political economy. Osborne argues Adorno became, if anything, more of a Marxist over his career, even as he suspended rather than completed philosophy's traditional tasks. RELEVANCE: Provides a comprehensive, career-spanning account of exactly how Adorno selectively appropriates and transforms Marx's categories (value, fetishism, ideology) into the vocabulary of negative dialectics, central to the domain's account of the Frankfurt School's selective inheritance of historical materialism. POSITION: Argues for reading Adorno as an increasingly, not decreasingly, Marxist thinker whose negative dialectics is a dialectical appropriation rather than an abandonment of Marx's critique of political economy.},
    keywords = "Osborne, Adorno, Marx, negative-dialectics, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "This essay reconstructs the place of Marx's thought within Adorno's writings from his 1931 inaugural lecture to his famous 1962 seminar on Marx. It focuses on three areas: the critique and transformation of philosophy; the sociology of the commodification of art; and the social ontology of the objectivity of illusions, derived from the critique of political economy. Adorno, it argues, ended his academic life significantly more of a Marxist than he had entered it, leaving a legacy that was distinctive both for its dialectical appropriation of Marx's critique -- and suspended supersession -- of philosophy and for its philosophical interpretation of Marx's critique of political economy.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{jameson1990latemarxism,
    author = "Jameson, Fredric",
    title = "Late Marxism: Adorno, or, the Persistence of the Dialectic",
    year = "1990",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Jameson argues against postmodernist appropriations of Adorno that recruit him against Marxism's "grand narratives," contending instead that Adorno's contribution to Marxism was unique and indispensable: Adorno is read as the theorist of late capitalism whose dialectical thinking about totality (the administered world of late capitalist "identity") is inseparable from, not opposed to, a materialist analysis of capital. RELEVANCE: Offers a rival account to purely anti-Marxist or purely anti-totality readings of Adorno's critique of identity-thinking, insisting that Adorno's negative dialectics remains internal to, rather than a departure from, historical-materialist analysis of capitalism as a social totality. POSITION: Defends Adorno as persistently, if unorthodoxly, Marxist against postmodern readings that sever his critique of totality from historical materialism altogether.},
    keywords = "Jameson, Adorno, totality, dialectic, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{buckmorss1977originnegativedialectics,
    author = "Buck-Morss, Susan",
    title = "The Origin of Negative Dialectics: Theodor W. Adorno, Walter Benjamin, and the Frankfurt Institute",
    address = "New York",
    year = "1977",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Buck-Morss traces the genesis of Adorno's negative dialectics through his intellectual exchange with Benjamin and the Frankfurt Institute, arguing that Adorno's mature method of immanent, non-identical critique develops partly through his critical appropriation and partial rejection of Benjamin's more theological, image-based materialism. RELEVANCE: Establishes the direct intellectual link between Benjamin's and Adorno's divergent strategies for retaining a materialist analysis of history and culture without lapsing into either orthodox economism or theological messianism, connecting the domain's Benjamin and Adorno strands. POSITION: Genetic-intellectual-historical account of negative dialectics as emerging from, and in critical tension with, Benjamin's materialist aesthetics and philosophy of history.",
    keywords = "Buck-Morss, Adorno, Benjamin, negative-dialectics, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{kellner1984marcusecrisis,
    author = "Kellner, Douglas",
    title = "Marcuse and the Crisis of Marxism",
    address = "London",
    year = "1984",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Kellner situates Marcuse's entire trajectory -- from his early Heideggerian Marxism through Reason and Revolution, the integration of Freud in Eros and Civilization, and the one-dimensionality thesis of One-Dimensional Man -- as a sustained response to what Kellner calls the "crisis of Marxism": the repeated failure of Marx's predicted revolutionary proletarian subject to materialize in advanced capitalist societies, which drove Marcuse to supplement historical materialism with psychoanalytic and phenomenological resources. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's most sustained secondary account of Marcuse's specific strategy -- psychoanalytic integration and a technology/domination analysis of "advanced industrial society" -- for explaining and responding to the proletariat's failure to become a revolutionary subject, complementing Horkheimer's and Adorno's parallel but differently inflected responses to the same crisis. POSITION: Frames Marcuse's career-long project as a continuous, evolving attempt to rescue historical materialism's emancipatory core from the crisis precipitated by the absence of proletarian revolution.},
    keywords = "Kellner, Marcuse, crisis-of-Marxism, psychoanalysis, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    sep_context = {SEP "Herbert Marcuse" (sec. 1, Biography) relies on Kellner 1984 as its primary biographical source, including for Marcuse's early Marxist formation -- "the dynamics of capitalism and imperialism, as well as the failure of the German Revolution" (Kellner 1984: 17) -- and for Kellner's own interpretive claim that Marcuse's turn from art to philosophy "suggests that Marcuse was a bit skeptical about the power of art as a 'cognitive source of knowledge and as an instrument of personal liberation and social change'" (Kellner 1984: 36-37).}
}

@misc{althusser2005formarx,
    author = "Althusser, Louis",
    title = "For Marx",
    year = "2005",
    howpublished = {Verso, London. Translated by Ben Brewster; collects essays including "Contradiction and Overdetermination" (orig. 1962) and "Marxism and Humanism" (orig. 1963).},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This collection of essays written between 1960 and 1965 (including "On the Young Marx," "Contradiction and Overdetermination," and "Marxism and Humanism") lays out Althusser's case for an "epistemological break" separating the ideological, Hegelian-Feuerbachian humanism of Marx's early writings from the scientific theoretical framework of the mature works. The essay "Contradiction and Overdetermination" introduces overdetermination as the concept needed to think how economic, political, and ideological contradictions condense in a "ruptural unity" without any contradiction ever appearing in a "pure" state. RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of anti-humanist, anti- historicist structural Marxism and the direct counterpoint to the humanist, consciousness-centered readings of Marx (Lukács, Gramsci, Sartre) surveyed elsewhere in this review. The "epistemological break" thesis and overdetermination are the conceptual starting points for every later reformulation of base/superstructure in this domain. POSITION: Founding statement of Althusserian structuralist Marxism and theoretical anti-humanism.},
    keywords = "althusser, anti-humanism, epistemological-break, overdetermination, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{althusserbalibar2016readingcapital,
    author = "Althusser, Louis and Balibar, Étienne",
    title = "Reading Capital",
    year = "2016",
    howpublished = "Verso, London. Translated by Ben Brewster (complete edition, with contributions of Establet, Macherey, and Rancière restored); French original: Lire le Capital, Maspero, 1965.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Originally the product of a 1965 collective seminar (also including Roger Establet, Pierre Macherey, and Jacques Rancière), this volume performs a "symptomatic reading" of Marx's Capital aimed at recovering the specifically Marxist theoretical concepts — value, the mode of production, the structure/effects relation — that Marx himself, on Althusser's account, did not fully theorize. It is here that "structural causality" is elaborated as the causal model proper to a "structure of structures" without center or telos, replacing both mechanistic and expressive (Hegelian) causality; Balibar's contribution develops the theory of modes of production and the concept of historical transition. RELEVANCE: This is the technical core of the structuralist reconstruction of historical materialism and the primary source for "structural causality," the central concept this domain must define against mechanistic/economic determinism. Balibar's chapter is the domain's main source for how structuralist Marxism theorizes transitions between modes of production, a question left largely implicit in Althusser's own essays. POSITION: Founding text of structuralist Marxism's theory of structural causality and modes of production.},
    keywords = "althusser, balibar, structural-causality, modes-of-production, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{althusser2002ideology,
    author = "Althusser, Louis",
    title = "Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses: Notes Towards an Investigation",
    year = "2002",
    howpublished = "In Lenin and Philosophy and Other Essays (Monthly Review Press, New York). Translated by Ben Brewster; French original: La Pensée 151 (1970).",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Ideology is theorized not as false consciousness but as a set of material practices and institutions — Ideological State Apparatuses (school, family, church, media, law) — that, unlike the coercive Repressive State Apparatus, secure the reproduction of the relations of production by "interpellating" individuals as subjects who recognize themselves in ideology. The essay reformulates base/superstructure via "relative autonomy": the ISAs are relatively autonomous from the economic base while still functioning, in the last instance, to reproduce it. RELEVANCE: This is the primary source for the domain's account of ideology and relative autonomy, and the essay whose functionalist cast (ideology as guaranteed reproduction) draws the sharpest criticism in the secondary literature (Rehmann, Bargu) for underestimating struggle and contingency in reproduction. POSITION: Founding statement of the ISA theory of ideology and relative autonomy of the ideological instance.},
    keywords = "althusser, ideology, ideological-state-apparatuses, relative-autonomy, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{poulantzas1973politicalpower,
    author = "Poulantzas, Nicos",
    title = "Political Power and Social Classes",
    year = "1973",
    howpublished = "NLB, London. Translated by Timothy O'Hagan; French original: Pouvoir Politique et Classes Sociales, François Maspero, 1968.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Applying Althusserian structuralist categories to the state, Poulantzas argues that the capitalist state is not a simple instrument wielded by the dominant class but a "factor of cohesion" of the social formation as a whole, whose "relative autonomy" from the immediate interests of any particular fraction of capital is precisely what allows it to organize the long-term political unity of the power bloc and to manage the balance of class forces, including through concessions to the dominated classes. RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of structuralist state theory and the primary source for the domain's question about how Poulantzas theorizes "relative autonomy" of the capitalist state; it also set the terms (structuralism versus instrumentalism) for the subsequent Miliband-Poulantzas debate treated in Sorokin (2025). POSITION: Founding statement of structuralist Marxist state theory.},
    sep_context = {Cited in the 'gramsci' SEP entry: "Marxist sociologists such as Nicos Poulantzas (1968 [1973]) and, later, Bob Jessop (1990) found in hegemony a resource to explore the permutations of the capitalist state and its shifting class coalitions."},
    keywords = "poulantzas, relative-autonomy, capitalist-state, structuralism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{poulantzas1978statepowersocialism,
    author = "Poulantzas, Nicos",
    title = "State, Power, Socialism",
    year = "1978",
    howpublished = "NLB, London.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Poulantzas revises his earlier, more rigidly structuralist account of the state, redescribing it as a "material condensation" of a relationship of forces among classes and class fractions rather than a functional apparatus guaranteeing cohesion. The book engages seriously, under increasing Gramscian influence, with the strategic question of a "democratic road to socialism" that neither abandons representative institutions nor treats the state as simply seizable. RELEVANCE: Shows the trajectory of structuralist state theory moving toward the categories (hegemony, condensation of forces) associated with the humanist/Gramscian tradition surveyed elsewhere in this review, complicating a simple structuralism/humanism binary within Poulantzas's own work — a shift analyzed directly in Sotiris (2017). POSITION: Poulantzas's late, Gramscian-inflected revision of structuralist state theory.},
    keywords = "poulantzas, state-theory, hegemony, democratic-socialism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{thompson1978povertyoftheory,
    author = "Thompson, E. P.",
    title = "The Poverty of Theory, or an Orrery of Errors",
    year = "1978",
    howpublished = "In The Poverty of Theory and Other Essays (Merlin, London).",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Thompson indicts Althusserian "Theoretical Practice" as a closed, self-authenticating system that eliminates any independent check by historical evidence, treats human agents as mere "bearers" of structures, and — despite its anti-Stalinist self-presentation — reproduces the theoretical authoritarianism of Stalinist orthodoxy in a new idiom. Against structural causality and overdetermination, Thompson counterposes "experience" as the category through which determinate historical pressures are lived, contested, and transformed into class consciousness and practice. RELEVANCE: This is the primary source for the domain's central backlash against structuralist Marxism and the direct counterpoint to Althusser's anti-historicism; it is also the pivot connecting this domain back to the humanist, experience- and agency-centered Western Marxism (Thompson's own historiographical practice descends from a culturalist reading of the same tradition as Lukács and Gramsci). POSITION: Founding polemic of the humanist/historicist critique of structuralist Marxism.},
    keywords = "thompson, poverty-of-theory, experience, anti-structuralism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{callinicos1976althussersmarxism,
    author = "Callinicos, Alex",
    title = "Althusser's Marxism",
    year = "1976",
    howpublished = "Pluto Press, London.",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: An early, sympathetic-but-critical exposition and assessment of Althusser's theoretical apparatus (epistemological break, structural causality, ideology) written soon after the English reception of For Marx and Reading Capital, situating Althusser's project within debates then current in Marxist philosophy of science and against rival humanist readings of Marx. RELEVANCE: Represents the first wave of Anglophone critical engagement with Althusserian Marxism, contemporaneous with the Thompson controversy, and is listed among the secondary literature the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy identifies as foundational to Althusser scholarship. POSITION: Early Trotskyist-inflected critical exposition of Althusserian Marxism.",
    keywords = "althusser, exposition, early-reception, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{benton1984risefall,
    author = "Benton, Ted",
    title = "The Rise and Fall of Structural Marxism: Althusser and His Influence",
    year = "1984",
    howpublished = "Macmillan, London.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the trajectory of structuralist Marxism from its rise in the 1960s through its fragmentation and decline by the early 1980s, assessing Althusser's own theoretical revisions and self-criticisms alongside the divergent paths taken by his students and collaborators (including Poulantzas and Balibar), and evaluating the internal tensions (functionalism, the status of "theoretical practice") that contributed to the current's dissolution. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's most direct historical account of why and how structuralist Marxism lost its dominant position, giving the review's overall arc (structuralism as a "current" rather than a permanent settlement) an empirical, historiographical anchor. POSITION: Retrospective critical history of the rise and decline of structuralist Marxism.},
    keywords = "althusser, structural-marxism, historiography, decline, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{jessop1990statetheory,
    author = "Jessop, Bob",
    title = "State Theory: Putting the Capitalist State in Its Place",
    year = "1990",
    howpublished = "Polity Press, Cambridge.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Surveys and synthesizes competing Marxist and non- Marxist theories of the capitalist state — including instrumentalist, structuralist (Poulantzas), and regulation-theoretic approaches — and develops a "strategic-relational" approach that treats the state as a site and product of ongoing struggles rather than a functionally guaranteed structure. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's principal synthetic overview of how Poulantzasian structuralist state theory relates to, and was eventually superseded by, later "relational" approaches to the state, situating the relative-autonomy thesis within the broader field of Marxist state theory. POSITION: Synthetic, strategic-relational successor theory to Poulantzasian structuralist state theory.},
    sep_context = {Cited in the 'gramsci' SEP entry alongside Poulantzas as part of the sociological tradition that "found in hegemony a resource to explore the permutations of the capitalist state and its shifting class coalitions."},
    keywords = "jessop, state-theory, relative-autonomy, synthesis, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{sotiris2014rethinkingstructure,
    author = "Sotiris, Panagiotis",
    title = "Rethinking Structure and Conjuncture in Althusser",
    year = "2014",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Althusser's evolving treatment of the relation between "structure" and "conjuncture," arguing that Althusser's later, less-discussed writings (including the "aleatory materialism" of the encounter) already contain resources for rethinking structural causality in a less rigidly systemic and more historically open direction than the classic Reading Capital-era formulations suggest. RELEVANCE: Offers a close reading of exactly the concept this domain must characterize — structural causality — and its internal tensions, useful for showing that "structuralism" in Althusser is not a static or monolithic position but one that shifts across his career. POSITION: Internal, developmental reconstruction of Althusserian structural causality against static readings of "structuralism."},
    abstract = "The relation between structure and conjuncture has been one of the biggest challenges facing social theory and Louis Althusser's writings provide some of the most important interventions on this subject. Contrary to an image of Althusser first embracing and then abandoning structuralism, Althusser tried from the beginning to articulate the theory of structural causality with an insistence on the singularity of historical conjunctures. Althusser's theoretical trajectory, despite his shortcomings, still offers a necessary starting point for a materialist conception of the relation between structural and conjunctural determinations that stresses the complex, uneven and overdetermined character of social reality without resorting either to a 'surface phenomena/deep structures' dualism or to the empiricism of simply registering singular practices.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    keywords = "althusser, structural-causality, conjuncture, aleatory-materialism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{pippa2016necessitycontingency,
    author = "Pippa, Stefano",
    title = "The Necessity of Contingency: Rereading Althusser on Structural Causality",
    year = "2016",
    howpublished = "Radical Philosophy 199: 15--25.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that structural causality — Althusser's term for the concept condensing "Marx's immense theoretical revolution" — breaks with both the mechanistic causality inherited from Descartes and the expressive causality inherited from Leibniz and Hegel, making it possible for the first time to think history as a process without essence, telos, subject, or center: a "structure of structures." The paper traces how this anti-humanist reading, first worked out via a Spinozist detour, both enabled and constrained Althusser's later thought. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's clearest single statement of what "structural causality" is and precisely how it differs from mechanistic/economic determinism, directly answering one of the domain's key questions. POSITION: Analytical reconstruction of structural causality as Althusser's alternative to mechanistic and expressive causality.},
    abstract = "Among the concepts proposed by Althusser in the course of his famous symptomatic reading of Marx's Capital, structural causality plays a central role. Extrapolated from Marx's writings via a detour through the philosophy of Spinoza, it came to represent the concept in which Althusser summed up 'Marx's immense theoretical revolution'. As is well known, for Althusser, by breaking with the other two models of causality available in Marx's time (the mechanistic causality derived from Descartes and the 'expressive' causality derived from Leibniz and above all Hegel), 'structural causality' made it possible for the first time to think of history as a process deprived of any essence and telos, without subject and without end -- or, as Althusser puts it in another formulation, as a 'structure of structures' without any centre. It was, therefore, the concept that condensed the anti-humanist reading produced in the 1960s by Althusser and his collaborators, one that soon became famous under the label 'structural Marxism'.",
    abstract_source = "core",
    keywords = "althusser, structural-causality, spinoza, anti-humanism, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, pages"
}

@misc{brown2021criterionimmanence,
    author = "Brown, Nathan",
    title = "The Criterion of Immanence and the Transformation of Structural Causality: From Althusser to Théorie Communiste",
    year = "2021",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Beginning from a critique of Althusser's reliance on the "structured whole" as a background totality, this chapter argues that Althusser's own commitment to the immanence of historical causality is compromised by residual holism, and reconstructs how the ultra-left theoretical current Théorie Communiste revised structural causality through a "theory of rift" that dispenses with any reference to a totalizing whole. RELEVANCE: Extends the domain's treatment of structural causality beyond Althusser's own texts to show how later Marxist currents pushed the anti-humanist, anti-teleological logic of structural causality further than Althusser himself was willing to go. POSITION: Post-Althusserian radicalization of structural causality against residual holism in Althusser's own formulations.},
    abstract = {Setting out from a critique of Althusser's reliance upon references to the "structured whole," Chapter 9 pursues a revision of the Althusserian theory of structural causality through the work of the group Théorie Communiste. Disentangling Althusser's concept from its Spinozist framework, I show that Althusser's references to the whole compromise his emphasis on the immanence of historical causality. I reconstruct an approach to the relation between immanence and lack through Théorie Communiste's "theory of rift." In the process, I engage with Jacques Rancière and Paul Mattick to assess the difference between humanist and anti-humanist approaches to proletarian revolution, and the chapter concludes with a consideration of Althusser's influence upon Théorie Communiste.},
    abstract_source = "s2",
    keywords = "althusser, structural-causality, theorie-communiste, anti-humanism, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{fuchs2019revisitingalthusser,
    author = "Fuchs, Christian",
    title = "Revisiting the Althusser/E. P. Thompson-Controversy: Towards a Marxist Theory of Communication",
    year = "2019",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Revisits the Althusser-Thompson controversy as a disagreement over how to relate the economic and non-economic (the base/superstructure problem) and over the relation of structure to agency: Althusser stresses articulation and overdetermination, Thompson stresses lived experience in class societies. Fuchs draws out implications of this disagreement for a Marxist theory of communication, arguing that communication mediates precisely the structure/agency dialectic at stake in the controversy. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the domain's key question about the Thompson-led backlash, and productively reframes the structuralist/ humanist opposition as a live methodological choice rather than a settled dispute, useful for the review's synthesis of how the debate has been received since. POSITION: Reconstructive, communication-theoretic reassessment of the Althusser/Thompson controversy.",
    abstract = "This essay revisits the controversy between Louis Althusser's Marxist structuralism and Edward P. Thompson's Marxist humanism. It draws conclusions from this controversy for the foundations of the Marxist theory of communication. The controversy's key disagreements concern the questions of how the economic and the non-economic (the base/superstructure problem), as well as structures and agency are related. Whereas Althusser focuses on articulation and over-determination, Thompson stresses the role of experience in society in general, and class societies in particular. This essay reflects on how both these approaches relate to the role of communication in society and capitalism. Communication is a process in which humans produce and reproduce social relations in manners that mediate not just understanding the world and other humans, but also the dialectic of structure and agency and the dialectic of society's realms. For a Marxist theory of society, also the forgotten meaning of communication as commoning is of crucial importance.",
    abstract_source = "core",
    keywords = "althusser, thompson, structure-agency, base-superstructure, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{jessop2017poulantzaspoliticaleconomy,
    author = "Jessop, Bob",
    title = "Nicos Poulantzas on Political Economy, Political Ecology, and Democratic Socialism",
    year = "2017",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Poulantzas's later views on the critique of political economy, arguing that his structuralist analysis of the capitalist state and social formation, though neglecting ecological questions typical of the 1970s French and Greek left, can be extended into a "Poulantzasian" political ecology consistent with his vision of democratic socialism and his emphasis on the relative autonomy and strategic character of the state. RELEVANCE: Extends the domain's coverage of Poulantzas's state theory into his less-discussed late work on political economy, reinforcing how "relative autonomy" functions as a strategic rather than merely descriptive category in his mature thought. POSITION: Sympathetic extension of Poulantzasian state theory into political ecology.},
    abstract = "This article presents the inaugural memorial lecture at the Nicos Poulantzas Institute in Athens. It examines and extends the work of the eponymous Greek legal and political theorist, political economist, and communist intellectual, Nicos Poulantzas, who radically transformed Marxist state theory, made major contributions to the critique of political economy for the era of Atlantic Fordism and post-war American imperialism, and called for a judicious balance between representative and direct democracy to secure a democratic transition to democratic socialism. It first offers some general reflections on the originality, legacy and actuality of Poulantzas's work in these respects and then reconstructs his later views on the critique of political economy before his death in 1979. Noting his neglect of the environment and issues of political ecology, which was typical of the French and Greek left in the 1970s and also rooted in more general features of Marxist theorizing on nature and the environment, the article elaborates a Poulantzasian view of political ecology based on key arguments from his work.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    keywords = "poulantzas, political-economy, democratic-socialism, relative-autonomy, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{sorokin2025milibandpoulantzas,
    author = "Sorokin, Egor M.",
    title = "The Place and Role of the State in a Capitalist Society: The Miliband – Poulantzas Debate",
    year = "2025",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes the 1969–1976 New Left Review polemic between Ralph Miliband and Nicos Poulantzas over the nature of the capitalist state, contrasting Miliband's instrumentalist emphasis on elites and institutional linkages with Poulantzas's structural emphasis on the state's relative autonomy as a "condenser" of class forces, and arguing that the exchange remains a significant reference point for understanding how the state mediates class contradictions in contemporary capitalism. RELEVANCE: Provides a direct secondary account of the instrumentalist/ structuralist opposition that Poulantzas's relative-autonomy thesis was designed to resolve, giving the domain a citable synthesis of the debate given that the original Miliband-Poulantzas exchange itself was not independently retrievable as a discrete item. POSITION: Retrospective synthesis of the instrumentalist/structuralist debate on the capitalist state.},
    abstract = {The article is devoted to an analysis of the pivotal 1970s polemic between R. Miliband and N. Poulantzas concerning the nature of the state in capitalist society. The author meticulously examines Miliband's instrumentalist approach, which emphasizes the role of elites and institutional linkages in securing bourgeois dominance, alongside Poulantzas's structural approach, which highlights the state's relative autonomy as a "condenser" of class forces and underscores the paramount influence of structures in class relations. The debate, unfolded in the journal New Left Review (articles from 1969-1976), is presented across four "rounds": from Poulantzas's critique of Miliband's empiricism to mutual accusations of abstraction and reductionism. The analysis draws on primary sources and secondary literature (Jessop, Barrow, Wright), which underscore that the exchanges, notwithstanding the parties' limited efforts to engage with opponents' arguments, constitute a significant milestone in intellectual history and have made a substantial contribution to the advancement of Marxist state theory. The conclusions emphasize the debate's ongoing relevance for comprehending contemporary capitalism, wherein the state still mediates class contradictions.},
    abstract_source = "s2",
    keywords = "poulantzas, miliband, relative-autonomy, capitalist-state, instrumentalism, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{sotiris2017althusserpoulantzas,
    author = "Sotiris, Panagiotis",
    title = "Althusser and Poulantzas: Hegemony and the State",
    year = "2017",
    howpublished = "Materialismo Storico 2 (1): 115--163.",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Retraces Althusser's and Poulantzas's respective encounters with Gramsci on hegemony and state theory, arguing that Althusser drew on Gramsci for the concept of Ideological State Apparatuses but underestimated the non-idealist, non-teleological character of Gramscian historicism, while Poulantzas's engagement with Gramsci deepened over time even as his explicit statements about Gramsci grew more critical the closer his own categories converged with Gramsci's notion of the "integral state." RELEVANCE: Directly addresses how Poulantzas's structuralism relates to, and partially converges with, the Gramscian tradition surveyed elsewhere in the review, complicating any simple opposition between "structuralist" and "humanist" Western Marxism at the level of state theory specifically. POSITION: Reconstructive account of the Althusser-Poulantzas-Gramsci relationship on hegemony and the state.},
    abstract = "The aim of this paper is to examine Althusser's and Poulantzas's confrontation with the work of Antonio Gramsci regarding in particular questions of hegemony and State theory. I try to retrace their various encounters and missed encounters with the work of Gramsci in order to show both the profound influence it had upon them but also how they both in fact misinterpreted crucial aspects of Gramsci's work. Regarding Althusser, I stress the influence Gramsci had upon his conceptualization of the Ideological Apparatuses of the State but also his inability to discern the non idealist and non teleological character of Gramsci's historicism and his tendency to underestimate the complexity of the notion of hegemony and Gramsci's conception of the integral State. Moreover, I try to see this in the context of Althusser's interventions in the debates on the State and strategy of the 1970s. Regarding Poulantzas, I stress his continuing dialogue with Gramsci, and how Gramsci influenced his theory of the State. I also stress that one of the contradictions of Poulantzas's work was that the closer he came to thematic and conceptualization that were compatible with Gramsci's theory of hegemony and the integral State, the more critical he tended to be towards Gramsci. Based on this elaboration, I try to stress the need for new theorizations of both State and strategy based upon both Gramsci and the work of Althusser and Poulantzas.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    keywords = "althusser, poulantzas, gramsci, hegemony, state-theory, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: doi"
}

@misc{rehmann2013althusserideological,
    author = "Rehmann, Jan",
    title = "Louis Althusser: Ideological State-Apparatuses and Subjection",
    year = "2013",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Situates Althusser's ISA theory as an elaboration of Gramsci's analysis of civil society and hegemonic apparatuses, arguing that Althusser's "point of view of reproduction" — his account of how the Ideological State Apparatuses secure ideological subjection under the "shield" of the repressive apparatus — has been widely and justly criticized as a functionalism that views social institutions from above and disregards the actual contradictions and struggles that take place within them. RELEVANCE: Directly documents the functionalism critique of Althusser's ISA theory that is one of the domain's key questions about structuralist Marxism's shortcomings, while also clarifying the theory's specific debt to Gramsci — a useful bridge to the humanist Western Marxist tradition surveyed elsewhere in the review. POSITION: Critical, Gramsci-informed exposition of Althusser's ISA theory and its functionalist limits.},
    abstract = "It is obvious that Louis Althusser based his ideology-theory on essential aspects of Gramsci's analyses of civil society and hegemonic apparatuses. The ideological state-apparatuses (ISA) reproduce the relations of production under the 'shield' (bouclier) of the repressive state-apparatuses. Althusser's main attention was directed to the ways in which the ISA achieve ideological subjection under the bourgeois state. The methodological point of departure for the ISA essay, first published in 1970, was the question concerning the 'reproduction of the conditions of production', that is, on the one hand, of the commodity of labour power, on the other, of the relations of production. Althusser's 'point of view of reproduction' has often been criticised as 'functionalism', that is, as a view from above that disregards the actual contradictions and struggles in social institutions.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    keywords = "althusser, ideological-state-apparatuses, functionalism, gramsci, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{nesbitt2017conceptincrisis,
    editor = "Nesbitt, Nick",
    title = "The Concept in Crisis: Reading Capital Today",
    year = "2017",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: This retrospective collection reconsiders Reading Capital's reading of Marx fifty years on, with contributors (including Alain Badiou, Étienne Balibar, and Fernanda Navarro) offering a symptomatic critique of Althusser, analyzing his writing as a materialist production of knowledge, examining the volume's conceptualization of value and crisis, and tracing the book's use by Latin American revolutionary leaders and its implications for feminist theory and praxis. RELEVANCE: Because Balibar's own contribution to Reading Capital is not separately available as a standalone secondary treatment in the sources searched, this volume — with a contribution by Balibar himself — is the domain's principal resource for reconstructing how the Reading Capital project, including Balibar's role in it, is being reassessed today. POSITION: Retrospective, multi-perspectival reassessment of the Reading Capital project's continuing relevance.",
    abstract = "The publication of Reading Capital -- by Louis Althusser, Etienne Balibar, Roger Establet, Pierre Macherey, and Jacques Ranciere -- in 1965 marked a key intervention in Marxist philosophy and critical theory, bringing forth a stunning array of concepts that continue to inspire philosophical reflection of the highest magnitude. The Concept in Crisis reconsiders the volume's reading of Marx and renews its call for a critique of capitalism and culture for the twenty-first century. The contributors -- who include Alain Badiou, Etienne Balibar, and Fernanda Navarro -- interrogate Althusser's contributions in particular within the context of what is surely the most famous collective reading of Marx ever undertaken. Among other topics, they offer a symptomatic critique of Althusser; consider his writing as a materialist production of knowledge; analyze the volume's conceptualization of value and crisis; examine how leftist Latin American leaders like Che Guevara and Subcomandante Marcos engaged with Althusser and Reading Capital; and draw out the volume's implications and use for feminist theory and praxis.",
    abstract_source = "s2",
    keywords = "althusser, balibar, reading-capital, retrospective, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc"
}

@book{cohen1978karl,
  author = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title = {Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton, NJ},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Marx's theory of history around two theses from
  the 1859 Preface: the Development Thesis (the productive forces have an
  autonomous tendency to develop throughout history) and the Primacy Thesis
  (the level of development of the productive forces explains the nature of
  the economic structure, i.e., the relations of production). Because Marx
  also holds that the relations of production control the development of the
  forces, Cohen argues the two claims are rendered consistent only by reading
  the primacy thesis as a functional explanation: relations of production
  persist, or are revolutionized, according to whether they promote or
  fetter the development of the productive forces, on the model of "birds
  have hollow bones because hollow bones facilitate flight."

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of analytical Marxism and the direct
  object of the entire debate this domain traces -- Elster's methodological-
  individualist critique, Wright/Levine/Sober's anti-reductionism, Wood's
  defense, and the Analyse & Kritik primacy-thesis exchange all take this
  functionalist reconstruction as their point of departure. An expanded 2000
  edition (Oxford: Clarendon Press) adds a new introduction and postscripts
  responding to Elster's game-theoretic alternative and to two decades of
  criticism.

  POSITION: Founding functionalist defense of historical materialism within
  analytical Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {functional-explanation, primacy-thesis, analytical-marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{elster1985making,
  author = {Elster, Jon},
  title = {Making Sense of Marx},
  year = {1985},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Applies methodological individualism and rational-choice/
  game-theoretic tools to reconstruct what remains defensible in Marx's
  thought. Macro-level social explanations -- including Cohen's functional
  explanations of historical materialism -- are acceptable, on Elster's
  view, only when microfoundations at the level of individual belief,
  desire, and action can at least be sketched; absent such microfoundations,
  "holist" Marxist explanations amount to an unexplained black box. Elster
  also assesses Marx's use of dialectic, distinguishing a wholly unpromising
  strand (dialectic as method of presentation) from more defensible strands
  (Engels's "dialectical laws" read as recurring patterns, and a theory of
  real social contradictions such as counterfinality).

  RELEVANCE: Sets out the systematic methodological-individualist alternative
  to Cohen's functionalism that structures this domain's central debate; its
  demand for microfoundations directly frames Wood's and Cohen's rejoinders
  and Wright/Levine/Sober's intermediate "anti-reductionist" position.

  POSITION: Founding methodological-individualist/rational-choice critique of
  functionalist historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {methodological-individualism, functional-explanation, rational-choice, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{wrightlevinesober1992reconstructing,
  author = {Wright, Erik Olin and Levine, Andrew and Sober, Elliott},
  title = {Reconstructing Marxism: Essays on Explanation and the Theory of History},
  year = {1992},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London and New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Develops an "anti-reductionist" position, distinct from both
  Cohen's functionalism and Elster's methodological individualism, holding
  that macro-level social explanations need not be reducible to individual-
  level microfoundations even though social entities are ultimately
  constituted by individuals. The authors distinguish the ontological claim
  that macro-social entities are constituted by individuals from the
  explanatory claim that macro-social facts must be explained in individual-
  level terms, and argue that facts realizable in multiple ways at the
  micro-level (their examples include "fittingness" in evolutionary biology
  and "economic survival" of capitalist firms) resist reduction to any single
  micro-level account, preserving the explanatory autonomy of macro-social
  theory.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the third major methodological position in this
  domain's central debate (alongside Cohen's functionalism and Elster's
  individualism), directly reshaping the terms in which the primacy of
  productive forces and technological determinism are assessed by denying
  that the dispute must be settled at the level of "rock-bottom"
  individual-level explanation.

  POSITION: Anti-reductionist alternative rejecting both strict functionalism
  and strict methodological individualism.
  },
  keywords = {anti-reductionism, methodological-individualism, class, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{elster1980cohen,
  author = {Elster, Jon},
  title = {Cohen on Marx's Theory of History},
  journal = {Political Studies},
  year = {1980},
  volume = {28},
  number = {1},
  pages = {121--128},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-9248.1980.tb01239.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: An early critical review of Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of
  History that inaugurates the functional-explanation debate between the two
  thinkers, questioning whether Cohen's functional reconstruction of the
  primacy of the productive forces can be sustained without individual-level
  mechanisms specifying how the relations of production come to track the
