  },
  keywords = {primacy-thesis, technological-determinism, class-struggle, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract This paper criticises the view that, according to Marx, “productive forces” determine “relations of production” and that the growth of the former basically determines the course of history. The particular version of this account discussed is that to be found in G.A. Cohen’s Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence. The main part of this criticism in:-volves a presentation of what, it is suggested, was in fact Marx's conception of "productive forces", "relations of production" and their relations, and an identification of class-struggle as the primary factor at least for the historically significant periods with which Marx was concerned.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{cohen1983reply,
  author = {Cohen, Gerald A.},
  title = {Reply to Four Critics},
  journal = {Analyse & Kritik},
  year = {1983},
  volume = {5},
  number = {2},
  pages = {195--222},
  doi = {10.1515/auk-1983-0203},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Responds to Philippe Van Parijs, Wal Suchting, Milton Fisk,
  and Steven Lukes. Cohen first clarifies an ambiguity in an argument for
  historical materialism presented in Karl Marx's Theory of History; against
  Van Parijs, he contends that historical materialism is consistent only if
  it explains production relations functionally, by reference to their
  propensity to develop the productive forces; against Suchting and Fisk, he
  rebuts the claim that class struggle plays a larger explanatory role in
  historical materialism than he assigns it; and he defends the doctrine of
  base and superstructure against Lukes's skepticism.

  RELEVANCE: Cohen's most concentrated single response to the primacy-thesis
  critics central to this domain, directly addressing whether the primacy
  thesis survives sustained analytical scrutiny.

  POSITION: Comprehensive defense of the functional primacy thesis against
  textual, coherence, and class-struggle-based objections.
  },
  keywords = {primacy-thesis, functional-explanation, class-struggle, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This article is a response to criticisms of my book on Karl Marx’s Theory of History which were made by four authors in last December’s number of Analyse & Kritik. After clarifying (section 2) an ambiguity in an argument for historical materialism which is presented in the book , I contend (3-5), against objections raised by Philippe Van Parijs , that historical materialism is consistent only if it explains production relations functionally, by reference to their propensity to develop the productive forces. Next (6-8) I address and rebut the views of Wal Suchting and Milton Fisk , who both think that the role of class struggle in historical materialism is larger than the one I assign to it. Finally (9-12) I try to vindicate the doctrine of base and superstructure proposed in my book against the skepticism of Steven Lukes.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{vanparijs1983shilling,
  author = {Van Parijs, Philippe},
  title = {The Shilling Primacy Puzzle. A Rejoinder},
  journal = {Analyse & Kritik},
  year = {1983},
  volume = {5},
  number = {2},
  pages = {223--230},
  doi = {10.1515/auk-1983-0204},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Cohen's "Reply to Four Critics" does not
  salvage the claim that only a functional interpretation of historical
  materialism can solve the "primacy puzzle" -- reconciling the primacy of
  the productive forces with the controlling role of the relations of
  production. Van Parijs contends Cohen avoids falsehood only by
  substantially redefining the terms of the puzzle, after which the claim
  becomes trivial, and proposes a "Veblenian scenario" as an alternative,
  non-functionalist solution to what he takes to be the only genuine primacy
  puzzle.

  RELEVANCE: Shows the primacy-thesis debate continuing past Cohen's 1983
  reply, testing directly whether functional explanation is indispensable to
  resolving the primacy puzzle.

  POSITION: Denies that functional explanation is required to reconcile
  forces/relations primacy; offers an alternative non-functionalist
  solution.
  },
  keywords = {primacy-thesis, functional-explanation, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract One important claim of G.A. Cohen's Karl Marx’s Theory of History is that only its functional interpretation of historical materialism can solve the “primacy puzzle”, i.e. can reconcile the primacy of the productive forces with the controlling role of the production relations. Cohen’s recent “Reply to Four Critics” (in this journal) does not salvage this claim against my earlier critique that it is either false or trivial. He only avoids falsehood by substantially redefining the terms of the puzzle. And with the redefined puzzle, the claim becomes trivial in the sense that one of the two terms which the primacy puzzle consists in reconciling requires a functional interpretation on its own . The “Veblenian scenario” which I put forward in my earlier text and whose full force Cohen has been prevented from appreciating by two misunderstandings, illustrates what I claim to be the general solution to the only genuine primacy puzzle.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{miller1981productive,
  author = {Miller, Richard W.},
  title = {Productive Forces and the Forces of Change: A Review of Gerald A. Cohen, Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defense},
  journal = {The Philosophical Review},
  year = {1981},
  volume = {90},
  number = {1},
  pages = {91},
  doi = {10.2307/2184374},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Situates Cohen's book against a mid-century Anglo-American
  tradition (Acton, Berlin, Popper, Plamenatz) that dismissed Marx's
  theories as too confused, metaphysical, or obviously mistaken to merit
  further empirical investigation, crediting Cohen with turning that tide
  through analytically rigorous reconstruction; the review essay then
  probes whether the primacy-of-productive-forces thesis can actually bear
  the explanatory weight Cohen places on it.

  RELEVANCE: An early and substantive assessment of the primacy thesis's
  analytical defensibility, directly feeding this domain's key question of
  whether the thesis survives scrutiny.

  POSITION: Critical-sympathetic assessment of Cohen's technological reading
  of historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {primacy-thesis, technological-determinism, Medium},
  abstract = {In the fifties and sixties, the point of most Anglo-American Marx studies was to show that Marx's theories were not worth studying, except as historically important relics. Acton, Berlin, Popper, and Plamenetz are just a few of those who argued that Marx's ideas were too confused, unclear, metaphysical, or obviously mistaken to merit further empirical investigation. In philosophy, Gerald Cohen has been a leader, probably the leader, in turning this tide. In a series of articles written over the last ten years, he has defined and defended Marx's views with the analytic resourcefulness of the best Anglo-American philosophy, and with grace, wit, and good humor that are altogether rare. In his articles, he was necessarily discussing small fragments of Marx's extremely synoptic social theory. His book on Marx's general theory of society and social change, Marx's Theory of History: A Defense, ' has, therefore, been eagerly awaited. Cohen's skill, style, and influence make it likely that his book will be the most widely read sympathetic exposition of Marx, among philosophers, for the next several years. Two groups of readers will find it of special use. Those who fear that sympathetic discussions of Marx must make his theories obscure or utterly implausible, or, otherwise, reduce them to platitudes will find this book an impressive counterexample. Those with their own interpretations of Marx will find that their thinking about him is enriched, clarified, and stimulated, even if their final thought about Cohen's book is that it is deeply wrong.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{shaw1986historical,
  author = {Shaw, William H.},
  title = {Historical Materialism and the Development Thesis},
  journal = {Philosophy of the Social Sciences},
  year = {1986},
  volume = {16},
